OCW.MIT.edu.

00:00:18.650 --> 00:00:21.510 align:middle line:90%


00:00:21.510 --> 00:00:25.670 align:middle line:84%
So some of my perspective on
what kinds of things you can

00:00:25.670 --> 00:00:27.930 align:middle line:84%
do in relation to game mechanics
is kind of like

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.660 align:middle line:90%
seeing [INAUDIBLE].

00:00:31.660 --> 00:00:34.932 align:middle line:84%
But it's not the be
all and end all.

00:00:34.932 --> 00:00:39.040 align:middle line:84%
I'm basically showing you my
perspective but it's not the

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.680 align:middle line:90%
only perspective out there.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:46.360 align:middle line:84%
I have very little that I feel
qualified to say about things

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:47.320 align:middle line:90%
like sports.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:53.246 align:middle line:84%
Things where we are talking
about athletic competitions.

00:00:53.246 --> 00:00:57.880 align:middle line:84%
Faster, higher, stronger,
that sort of thing.

00:00:57.880 --> 00:01:02.020 align:middle line:84%
But I'm a little bit more
engaged in games where there's

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:02.750 align:middle line:90%
role-playing.

00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:07.270 align:middle line:84%
Where you're taking on a persona
or at least some sort

00:01:07.270 --> 00:01:10.140 align:middle line:84%
of behavior that
you aren't just

00:01:10.140 --> 00:01:13.030 align:middle line:90%
normally doing in real-life.

00:01:13.030 --> 00:01:15.680 align:middle line:84%
It's not just about how fast you
can run or how skilled you

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:17.370 align:middle line:90%
are at hitting the ball.

00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:18.620 align:middle line:90%
That sort of thing.

00:01:18.620 --> 00:01:21.510 align:middle line:90%


00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:26.860 align:middle line:84%
Let's see, and not every single
live-action game needs

00:01:26.860 --> 00:01:29.090 align:middle line:84%
to necessarily fit within
role-playing sports.

00:01:29.090 --> 00:01:30.930 align:middle line:84%
You've got things like
party games.

00:01:30.930 --> 00:01:35.020 align:middle line:84%
This afternoon we'll have the
lead designer for Dance

00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:40.500 align:middle line:84%
central coming in to do a
little Q & A about Dance

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:44.330 align:middle line:84%
Central around 4 o'clock,
4:30, in the TV lounge.

00:01:44.330 --> 00:01:45.510 align:middle line:84%
You guys are all welcome
to do that.

00:01:45.510 --> 00:01:47.120 align:middle line:84%
And that's arguably a
live-action game.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:50.810 align:middle line:84%
Sure you've got a computer,
you've got a--

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.480 align:middle line:90%
how much is the Kinect?

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:55.880 align:middle line:90%


00:01:55.880 --> 00:02:05.400 align:middle line:84%
$150 piece of equipment
connected to a $250 Xbox

00:02:05.400 --> 00:02:08.259 align:middle line:84%
hooked to a screen that's
probably more than that.

00:02:08.259 --> 00:02:10.550 align:middle line:84%
You've got all this technology,
but in the end,

00:02:10.550 --> 00:02:12.150 align:middle line:90%
what's that game about?

00:02:12.150 --> 00:02:14.330 align:middle line:90%
What's Rock Band about?

00:02:14.330 --> 00:02:16.700 align:middle line:84%
It's about the people who
are in the living room.

00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:19.660 align:middle line:90%


00:02:19.660 --> 00:02:23.550 align:middle line:84%
And what is one of the arguments
that [INAUDIBLE], in

00:02:23.550 --> 00:02:29.730 align:middle line:84%
a different chapter in Casual
Revolution, he argues that one

00:02:29.730 --> 00:02:32.730 align:middle line:84%
of the big things that's
happened with gaming is this

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.500 align:middle line:84%
re-introduction of the space
in front of the screen.

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:40.485 align:middle line:84%
The living room space has
come back into play.

00:02:40.485 --> 00:02:43.470 align:middle line:90%


00:02:43.470 --> 00:02:46.050 align:middle line:84%
You haven't lost that with board
games or card games, but

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:48.800 align:middle line:84%
for electronic games really it
used to be something that

00:02:48.800 --> 00:02:50.300 align:middle line:90%
existed with arcade games.

00:02:50.300 --> 00:02:55.290 align:middle line:84%
You've got these giant
motorcycles or steering wheels

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:56.840 align:middle line:90%
and stuff like that.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:03:01.540 align:middle line:84%
And you still had it with some
really expensive computer

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:03.450 align:middle line:84%
games like flight
simulators where

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.840 align:middle line:90%
people buy the joysticks.

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:12.230 align:middle line:84%
I'm a big train fan, so I've
got a Playstation One train

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:14.380 align:middle line:90%
controller.

00:03:14.380 --> 00:03:15.720 align:middle line:90%
It's throttle and brakes.

00:03:15.720 --> 00:03:17.868 align:middle line:90%
That's all it is.

00:03:17.868 --> 00:03:20.730 align:middle line:84%
No steering when it
comes to trains.

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.980 align:middle line:90%
It's a lot of fun.

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:27.710 align:middle line:90%


00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.840 align:middle line:84%
With arcade games, and of course
[INAUDIBLE], there's

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.550 align:middle line:84%
this whole idea that you're
going to be engaging your

00:03:31.550 --> 00:03:34.660 align:middle line:90%
entire body.

00:03:34.660 --> 00:03:37.470 align:middle line:84%
And you could argue that those
are live-action games.

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:41.860 align:middle line:84%
And you take the technology
out and what you've got is

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:45.970 align:middle line:84%
just this human body playing
with all it's constraints,

00:03:45.970 --> 00:03:52.160 align:middle line:84%
beholden to gravity, it can't
move faster than a certain

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:55.415 align:middle line:84%
speed, it's kind of ungamely,
and it's got

00:03:55.415 --> 00:03:58.490 align:middle line:90%
roughly a certain size.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:01.200 align:middle line:84%
Gives you constraints that
you can work with in

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:02.980 align:middle line:90%
terms of game mechanics.

00:04:02.980 --> 00:04:06.470 align:middle line:84%
One of the things that we did
last year was we had a

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:09.850 align:middle line:84%
live-action game assignment
in this class.

00:04:09.850 --> 00:04:12.250 align:middle line:84%
And the constraint was you had
to be able to play the

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:13.500 align:middle line:90%
game in this room.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.040 align:middle line:90%


00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.720 align:middle line:90%
Was a newspaper a constant?

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:19.589 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: No.

00:04:19.589 --> 00:04:23.065 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That was one huge
decision to use newspaper as a

00:04:23.065 --> 00:04:24.900 align:middle line:90%
constraint.

00:04:24.900 --> 00:04:27.280 align:middle line:84%
But they did things like
blindfold people.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.780 align:middle line:84%
They were playing around with
the senses on the body so that

00:04:30.780 --> 00:04:32.270 align:middle line:90%
you could hear.

00:04:32.270 --> 00:04:35.500 align:middle line:84%
When one person had unlimited
ammunition, in terms of low

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:41.820 align:middle line:84%
vaulted newspaper and the
bucket, but couldn't see where

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:42.650 align:middle line:90%
everybody else was.

00:04:42.650 --> 00:04:47.500 align:middle line:84%
And everybody else could
move around the room

00:04:47.500 --> 00:04:48.530 align:middle line:90%
on sheets of newspaper.

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:50.480 align:middle line:84%
They had to step on sheets
of newspaper.

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:52.800 align:middle line:84%
So you could always hear where
they were stepping.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:56.440 align:middle line:84%
And the person with the bucket
was trying to pelt the people

00:04:56.440 --> 00:04:58.610 align:middle line:84%
who were stepping
on the ground.

00:04:58.610 --> 00:05:03.940 align:middle line:84%
If you got hit you had to go
out of the magic circle and

00:05:03.940 --> 00:05:05.780 align:middle line:90%
then start coming in.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:07.525 align:middle line:84%
The whole idea was to attack
the person who was actually

00:05:07.525 --> 00:05:09.330 align:middle line:84%
throwing all these
newspaper balls.

00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.720 align:middle line:84%
We had a little newspaper
hat that you could grab.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.880 align:middle line:84%
It was pretty awesome,
because what you need

00:05:14.880 --> 00:05:15.870 align:middle line:90%
to play that game?

00:05:15.870 --> 00:05:21.750 align:middle line:84%
You need one set of rules,
and a chair, a recycle

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:23.770 align:middle line:84%
bin--specifically it
was that bin--

00:05:23.770 --> 00:05:26.520 align:middle line:90%
and newspaper.

00:05:26.520 --> 00:05:28.780 align:middle line:84%
But there were all these
things in play about

00:05:28.780 --> 00:05:29.590 align:middle line:90%
what you can do.

00:05:29.590 --> 00:05:33.240 align:middle line:84%
So OK, your target is going to
be roughly the size of a human

00:05:33.240 --> 00:05:35.230 align:middle line:84%
body, and roughly the weight
of a human body.

00:05:35.230 --> 00:05:38.260 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be able to move at
roughly the same speed of a

00:05:38.260 --> 00:05:39.110 align:middle line:90%
human body.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:42.387 align:middle line:84%
And you cannot have multiple
targets in the

00:05:42.387 --> 00:05:43.420 align:middle line:90%
same place at once.

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:46.290 align:middle line:84%
In fact it's hilarious when you
try, because you've got

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.000 align:middle line:90%
these [INAUDIBLE].

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.080 align:middle line:84%
And because you're playing with
the humans, all five of

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:56.190 align:middle line:84%
the human senses, usually
just like hearing.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:57.650 align:middle line:84%
I don't know that anyone's
made a smell game.

00:05:57.650 --> 00:06:04.620 align:middle line:84%
But you will have the situation
where the people who

00:06:04.620 --> 00:06:08.710 align:middle line:84%
are standing on the newspapers
will try to make noise near

00:06:08.710 --> 00:06:10.520 align:middle line:84%
the other people who are
playing the game.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:15.280 align:middle line:84%
So they were drawing attention
away from themselves.

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:15.690 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it works.

00:06:15.690 --> 00:06:16.380 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it doesn't.

00:06:16.380 --> 00:06:18.406 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes it leads to hilarious
collisions --a good

00:06:18.406 --> 00:06:19.656 align:middle line:90%
time was had by all.

00:06:19.656 --> 00:06:21.460 align:middle line:90%


00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:22.470 align:middle line:84%
And those kinds of
party games.

00:06:22.470 --> 00:06:23.720 align:middle line:90%
You know, King of the Hill?

00:06:23.720 --> 00:06:28.430 align:middle line:90%


00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:33.240 align:middle line:84%
Can anyone think of any other
playground type games that are

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:33.930 align:middle line:90%
personal favorites?

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:35.085 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Freeze tag.

00:06:35.085 --> 00:06:36.330 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Freeze tag?

00:06:36.330 --> 00:06:36.453 align:middle line:90%
[?

00:06:36.453 --> 00:06:38.340 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Lava.

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:38.600 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: What's?

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:38.643 align:middle line:90%
[?

00:06:38.643 --> 00:06:42.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Lava's where you have
swings and all things you

00:06:42.190 --> 00:06:43.010 align:middle line:90%
can climb on.

00:06:43.010 --> 00:06:46.330 align:middle line:84%
And you're playing generally
on the little pebble-like

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:46.970 align:middle line:90%
rocky stuff.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:49.050 align:middle line:84%
And if you step on the pebble
stuff that's like the lava and

00:06:49.050 --> 00:06:51.390 align:middle line:90%
you get burnt.

00:06:51.390 --> 00:06:54.040 align:middle line:84%
So you have to stay hanging
on the metal part.

00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.650 align:middle line:84%
And usually there's someone
who's immune to the lava

00:06:56.650 --> 00:06:57.670 align:middle line:90%
trying to tag you.

00:06:57.670 --> 00:06:59.470 align:middle line:84%
So they can walk over
the lava but they

00:06:59.470 --> 00:07:01.480 align:middle line:90%
can't go in the structure.

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:04.020 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Lava monster.

00:07:04.020 --> 00:07:05.530 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Does the
game ever stop?

00:07:05.530 --> 00:07:07.580 align:middle line:90%
Just out of curiosity?

00:07:07.580 --> 00:07:09.990 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, someone becomes
the next lava monster.

00:07:09.990 --> 00:07:11.925 align:middle line:84%
Once you get tagged you switch
roles and keep doing it.

00:07:11.925 --> 00:07:14.748 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But the thing is,
I never played that on my

00:07:14.748 --> 00:07:15.150 align:middle line:90%
playground.

00:07:15.150 --> 00:07:17.640 align:middle line:84%
I always played in my living
room and throw couch cushions

00:07:17.640 --> 00:07:20.130 align:middle line:90%
on the floor and stuff.

00:07:20.130 --> 00:07:22.120 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That sounds
really dangerous.

00:07:22.120 --> 00:07:25.210 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We'd have wheely
chairs and push off and yeah.

00:07:25.210 --> 00:07:25.950 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I used to
have a water bed.

00:07:25.950 --> 00:07:29.034 align:middle line:84%
So there was some
unpredictability that I could

00:07:29.034 --> 00:07:32.010 align:middle line:84%
have in my bedroom with
my two brothers.

00:07:32.010 --> 00:07:34.320 align:middle line:84%
But they were both bigger than
me so I always lost.

00:07:34.320 --> 00:07:39.120 align:middle line:90%


00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.480 align:middle line:90%
I mean, you can think of--

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:45.690 align:middle line:84%
on one hand, these are athletic
games because they do

00:07:45.690 --> 00:07:49.300 align:middle line:84%
test your ability to move
and control your body.

00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Which is why they're so popular
around kids who are

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:55.590 align:middle line:84%
trying to master what their
bodies are capable of.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:08:00.160 align:middle line:84%
But on the other hand, they're
also games of mimicry going

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:01.748 align:middle line:90%
back to [INAUDIBLE]

00:08:01.748 --> 00:08:03.050 align:middle line:90%
definition.

00:08:03.050 --> 00:08:05.967 align:middle line:84%
Because you're pretending that
it's lava, and pretending that

00:08:05.967 --> 00:08:10.220 align:middle line:84%
you're a person that cares that
it's lava, or a monster

00:08:10.220 --> 00:08:12.160 align:middle line:90%
that's immune to it.

00:08:12.160 --> 00:08:15.730 align:middle line:84%
And these games don't
necessarily--

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:17.300 align:middle line:84%
they may have some rules
about, OK you

00:08:17.300 --> 00:08:19.440 align:middle line:90%
can tag now we swap.

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:23.830 align:middle line:84%
I've seen games where sure, I've
technically been tagged

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.945 align:middle line:84%
out of this game but since this
game really has no end

00:08:26.945 --> 00:08:29.440 align:middle line:84%
state that just means
that oh, I lost.

00:08:29.440 --> 00:08:31.180 align:middle line:84%
Now I'm back in the
game again.

00:08:31.180 --> 00:08:35.070 align:middle line:84%
It's like playing a Doom death
match and you die and you're

00:08:35.070 --> 00:08:38.390 align:middle line:84%
just right back in
the game again.

00:08:38.390 --> 00:08:42.490 align:middle line:90%
Let's see, anyone play those--

00:08:42.490 --> 00:08:44.750 align:middle line:84%
what's the name of the football
variant where you

00:08:44.750 --> 00:08:47.296 align:middle line:84%
just have a scarf
attached to you?

00:08:47.296 --> 00:08:48.546 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Flag football.

00:08:48.546 --> 00:08:51.070 align:middle line:90%


00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:53.485 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: For those of you--
everyone knows what it is?

00:08:53.485 --> 00:08:59.940 align:middle line:90%


00:08:59.940 --> 00:09:02.320 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Isn't flag football
where you just have a flag and

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:05.160 align:middle line:90%
instead of being tackled you?

00:09:05.160 --> 00:09:05.800 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Yeah, instead
of tackling you

00:09:05.800 --> 00:09:07.050 align:middle line:90%
just grab the flag.

00:09:07.050 --> 00:09:09.190 align:middle line:90%
And that's an abstraction.

00:09:09.190 --> 00:09:11.610 align:middle line:84%
That's an abstraction of
something that you well could

00:09:11.610 --> 00:09:13.452 align:middle line:90%
physically do.

00:09:13.452 --> 00:09:15.380 align:middle line:84%
You could grab this person and
pull them to the ground.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:17.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Does touch
football exist?

00:09:17.430 --> 00:09:18.680 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yes.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.490 align:middle line:90%


00:09:21.490 --> 00:09:23.230 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That's a smaller
level of abstraction because

00:09:23.230 --> 00:09:24.860 align:middle line:84%
you can really hit someone
pretty damn

00:09:24.860 --> 00:09:27.496 align:middle line:90%
hard with touch football.

00:09:27.496 --> 00:09:30.710 align:middle line:84%
But it's an abstraction of,
here's this thing that I

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:32.430 align:middle line:84%
personally, physically,
could do.

00:09:32.430 --> 00:09:33.780 align:middle line:84%
But I'm going to introduce
this level

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:34.740 align:middle line:90%
of abstraction for--

00:09:34.740 --> 00:09:36.970 align:middle line:90%
I don't know-- safety.

00:09:36.970 --> 00:09:39.320 align:middle line:90%
That's usually the reason.

00:09:39.320 --> 00:09:44.090 align:middle line:84%
And so you've got the human body
and you've got all of its

00:09:44.090 --> 00:09:48.010 align:middle line:84%
values and virtues and
all of its curses.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:49.260 align:middle line:90%
You've got issues of safety.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.915 align:middle line:90%


00:09:51.915 --> 00:09:56.080 align:middle line:84%
You don't want people to hurt
each other, but yet human

00:09:56.080 --> 00:09:59.073 align:middle line:84%
beings and their bodies are
extremely capable of hurting

00:09:59.073 --> 00:10:02.450 align:middle line:84%
each other with no added
implement at all.

00:10:02.450 --> 00:10:05.771 align:middle line:84%
Even worse when it gets to a
game and someone's trying to

00:10:05.771 --> 00:10:07.850 align:middle line:90%
win something.

00:10:07.850 --> 00:10:10.860 align:middle line:84%
If you don't have a specific
rule that says you can't punch

00:10:10.860 --> 00:10:14.540 align:middle line:84%
this person, but you have a
game that's very athletic,

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:15.830 align:middle line:90%
someone's going to try.

00:10:15.830 --> 00:10:21.330 align:middle line:84%
That's why you get these sort of
abstractions to pretend in

00:10:21.330 --> 00:10:23.740 align:middle line:84%
that military form that all
right, I've grabbed

00:10:23.740 --> 00:10:24.650 align:middle line:90%
this thing off you.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:26.594 align:middle line:90%
Now you have just been tagged.

00:10:26.594 --> 00:10:31.380 align:middle line:84%
I've effectively tackled
you [INAUDIBLE].

00:10:31.380 --> 00:10:36.170 align:middle line:84%
So, what I'm trying to draw
attention to with these

00:10:36.170 --> 00:10:42.520 align:middle line:84%
examples is that with
live-actions games the thing

00:10:42.520 --> 00:10:47.620 align:middle line:84%
that you have to keep in mind
all the time is at what point

00:10:47.620 --> 00:10:51.110 align:middle line:84%
is the person who's playing
actually the

00:10:51.110 --> 00:10:52.780 align:middle line:90%
character who's playing?

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:55.150 align:middle line:84%
It's really, really, hard
to tell when it comes to

00:10:55.150 --> 00:10:56.210 align:middle line:90%
live-action games.

00:10:56.210 --> 00:11:00.820 align:middle line:84%
If you are playing in sports
it's the same thing.

00:11:00.820 --> 00:11:04.010 align:middle line:90%


00:11:04.010 --> 00:11:09.010 align:middle line:84%
Any given athlete and their
performance in the sport is

00:11:09.010 --> 00:11:09.870 align:middle line:90%
who they are.

00:11:09.870 --> 00:11:11.320 align:middle line:90%
It's the same person.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:14.770 align:middle line:84%
If I'm role-playing then the
person that I'm playing is not

00:11:14.770 --> 00:11:15.820 align:middle line:90%
supposed to be the same.

00:11:15.820 --> 00:11:19.360 align:middle line:84%
But there needs to be a whole
bunch of rules that basically

00:11:19.360 --> 00:11:23.680 align:middle line:84%
specify that the character
that I'm playing is not

00:11:23.680 --> 00:11:27.630 align:middle line:84%
actually the person that you're
seeing in front of you.

00:11:27.630 --> 00:11:33.214 align:middle line:84%
For that time, the player is not
the Count of Monte Cristo

00:11:33.214 --> 00:11:34.200 align:middle line:90%
or something like that.

00:11:34.200 --> 00:11:34.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: One interesting

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:36.180 align:middle line:90%
exception to that is wrestling.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:38.990 align:middle line:90%


00:11:38.990 --> 00:11:41.000 align:middle line:84%
Not wrestling like actual
high-school wrestling, but

00:11:41.000 --> 00:11:42.602 align:middle line:90%
wrestling on TV--

00:11:42.602 --> 00:11:43.360 align:middle line:84%
I don't remember what
it's called.

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:44.820 align:middle line:84%
Because everybody puts
on a persona.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:46.770 align:middle line:84%
They're never the same
person that they are.

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.475 align:middle line:84%
I just thought it was
interesting when you said that

00:11:49.475 --> 00:11:51.460 align:middle line:84%
any sports person, but
that's one exception.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.590 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: That's true.

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:56.890 align:middle line:84%
And that creates a lot of
discussion about whether

00:11:56.890 --> 00:11:59.971 align:middle line:90%
wrestling is a sport.

00:11:59.971 --> 00:12:00.840 align:middle line:90%
It's athletic.

00:12:00.840 --> 00:12:01.780 align:middle line:84%
Everyone agrees that
it's athletic.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.790 align:middle line:84%
But so are trapeze artists
athletic but you wouldn't call

00:12:04.790 --> 00:12:06.040 align:middle line:90%
it a sport.

00:12:06.040 --> 00:12:08.920 align:middle line:90%


00:12:08.920 --> 00:12:11.090 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, if I understand
correctly, wrestlers are

00:12:11.090 --> 00:12:15.185 align:middle line:84%
insured as circus performers,
because they have the same

00:12:15.185 --> 00:12:19.320 align:middle line:84%
risks and are playing the same
role as a performance.

00:12:19.320 --> 00:12:21.550 align:middle line:84%
But you're right, that's
one of those things

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:25.590 align:middle line:90%
where it's a persona.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:28.430 align:middle line:84%
The really, really, good
wrestling networks play around

00:12:28.430 --> 00:12:32.140 align:middle line:84%
with what's real
and what's not.

00:12:32.140 --> 00:12:35.430 align:middle line:84%
Yeah sure, these two people
are putting on these

00:12:35.430 --> 00:12:39.060 align:middle line:90%
ridiculous personalities.

00:12:39.060 --> 00:12:42.890 align:middle line:84%
Threatening to kill each other
over the most trivial and

00:12:42.890 --> 00:12:47.550 align:middle line:84%
made-up of the offenses, and
crazy accents, and costumes,

00:12:47.550 --> 00:12:48.200 align:middle line:90%
and everything.

00:12:48.200 --> 00:12:50.650 align:middle line:84%
But when they're bleeding
they're actually bleeding.

00:12:50.650 --> 00:12:55.400 align:middle line:84%
There's no denying and no doubt
they actually get angry

00:12:55.400 --> 00:12:58.980 align:middle line:84%
with each other and people
actually get hurt.

00:12:58.980 --> 00:13:02.040 align:middle line:84%
And playing around with that
line is actually some of the

00:13:02.040 --> 00:13:06.222 align:middle line:84%
appeal that we get in things
like professional wrestling.

00:13:06.222 --> 00:13:09.620 align:middle line:84%
I guess that's what the
terminology that they use.

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:11.270 align:middle line:84%
It's very different from
something like

00:13:11.270 --> 00:13:12.520 align:middle line:90%
mixed martial arts.

00:13:12.520 --> 00:13:16.340 align:middle line:90%


00:13:16.340 --> 00:13:18.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't watch mixed
martial arts.

00:13:18.080 --> 00:13:18.970 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: No, I don't think
so, I'm pretty sure

00:13:18.970 --> 00:13:21.180 align:middle line:90%
it's just flat out.

00:13:21.180 --> 00:13:24.070 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] are very
associated with the person.

00:13:24.070 --> 00:13:26.021 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So they
are the persona.

00:13:26.021 --> 00:13:27.197 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They don't have stage
personalities, but they

00:13:27.197 --> 00:13:29.350 align:middle line:90%
do have strong personalities.

00:13:29.350 --> 00:13:31.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's definitely
a sport.

00:13:31.180 --> 00:13:33.390 align:middle line:84%
I mean, they're actually
fighting.

00:13:33.390 --> 00:13:36.100 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I mean let's
take a look at

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.790 align:middle line:90%
Muhammad Ali for instance.

00:13:39.790 --> 00:13:42.460 align:middle line:84%
Extremely athletic person but
his personality had a lot to

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:45.620 align:middle line:84%
do with it as well with how
successful that he was.

00:13:45.620 --> 00:13:50.420 align:middle line:84%
The way that he taunted people
on TV and on cameras before

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:53.230 align:middle line:84%
the game, so that people would
get all worked up for the

00:13:53.230 --> 00:13:54.880 align:middle line:90%
game, made him a star.

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:58.420 align:middle line:84%
Sure he was athletic, but
that was part of it.

00:13:58.420 --> 00:14:04.430 align:middle line:84%
So where that line is, and as
designers you get some control

00:14:04.430 --> 00:14:07.715 align:middle line:84%
over that through the rules
that you're writing.

00:14:07.715 --> 00:14:13.040 align:middle line:84%
And there are a couple of
tactics that you can focus on.

00:14:13.040 --> 00:14:17.060 align:middle line:90%
But I want to segue into--

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:18.120 align:middle line:90%
that's the tension.

00:14:18.120 --> 00:14:20.050 align:middle line:84%
That is one thing that's
pulling your

00:14:20.050 --> 00:14:21.340 align:middle line:90%
design in one direction.

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:26.260 align:middle line:84%
Which is, how am I going to
figure out what the difference

00:14:26.260 --> 00:14:28.640 align:middle line:84%
between the person who's playing
this game and the

00:14:28.640 --> 00:14:30.290 align:middle line:84%
character that they're playing,
or the role that

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:33.030 align:middle line:84%
they're playing,
is going to be.

00:14:33.030 --> 00:14:36.830 align:middle line:84%
In your games, for this next
assignment, if you've got

00:14:36.830 --> 00:14:39.750 align:middle line:84%
people playing mental patients
then you've got to figure out

00:14:39.750 --> 00:14:41.600 align:middle line:84%
how you're going to
draw that line.

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:45.370 align:middle line:84%
You've got to be clear whether
it's the player who's actually

00:14:45.370 --> 00:14:47.080 align:middle line:84%
upset at you or the character
who's actually upset at you.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.030 align:middle line:90%


00:14:50.030 --> 00:14:52.840 align:middle line:84%
But if you draw that line too
broadly, then you've got the

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:56.310 align:middle line:84%
situation where both be angry
at you [INAUDIBLE].

00:14:56.310 --> 00:14:59.820 align:middle line:84%
Because this is a ridiculous
situation and emotional.

00:14:59.820 --> 00:15:04.570 align:middle line:84%
Pretending to be really
emotional can usually make a

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:09.340 align:middle line:84%
lot of people react by laughing
just to release the

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:12.326 align:middle line:90%
tension in space.

00:15:12.326 --> 00:15:16.380 align:middle line:84%
In fact, that happened when
Dion was role-playing with

00:15:16.380 --> 00:15:17.160 align:middle line:90%
Caesar, right?

00:15:17.160 --> 00:15:19.225 align:middle line:90%
Sure, they were laughing.

00:15:19.225 --> 00:15:21.320 align:middle line:84%
They were trying to level that
and take it as seriously as

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:25.640 align:middle line:84%
possible, and everybody else in
the room was cracking up.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:26.800 align:middle line:90%
To release that tension.

00:15:26.800 --> 00:15:29.850 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes that's
OK playing games.

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:31.160 align:middle line:84%
In fact, sometimes that's
great for the game.

00:15:31.160 --> 00:15:33.630 align:middle line:84%
And sometimes that's
detrimental.

00:15:33.630 --> 00:15:35.270 align:middle line:84%
Depends on what you're
playing for.

00:15:35.270 --> 00:15:38.580 align:middle line:84%
But you have to understand
that is something that's

00:15:38.580 --> 00:15:42.155 align:middle line:84%
pulling the overall design, why
do rules exist, and why

00:15:42.155 --> 00:15:44.870 align:middle line:84%
your game mechanics exist, and
why your game mechanic is

00:15:44.870 --> 00:15:46.040 align:middle line:90%
pretending a certain way.

00:15:46.040 --> 00:15:47.347 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the tensions
that's pulling it

00:15:47.347 --> 00:15:50.760 align:middle line:90%
in a certain direction.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.570 align:middle line:90%
And in reality, that's actually

00:15:52.570 --> 00:15:53.600 align:middle line:90%
two different tensions.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.900 align:middle line:84%
Because in one sense, they're
trying to make an experience

00:15:57.900 --> 00:16:02.020 align:middle line:84%
as aversive, as feeling
like you're

00:16:02.020 --> 00:16:04.450 align:middle line:90%
actually doing it as possible.

00:16:04.450 --> 00:16:07.240 align:middle line:84%
And on the other hand, you
don't want to make it so

00:16:07.240 --> 00:16:09.670 align:middle line:84%
associated with who you are,
and what you're actually

00:16:09.670 --> 00:16:12.120 align:middle line:84%
doing, that people start
to think, oh wow.

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:16.120 align:middle line:84%
This person, this player,
is actually mad at me.

00:16:16.120 --> 00:16:18.170 align:middle line:84%
Or, this player could actually
hit me at this point.

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:21.050 align:middle line:84%
While as, no the character
[INAUDIBLE].

00:16:21.050 --> 00:16:24.350 align:middle line:90%


00:16:24.350 --> 00:16:28.300 align:middle line:90%
I call that verisimilitude.

00:16:28.300 --> 00:16:32.540 align:middle line:84%
These are just words that I
picked up from dictionaries.

00:16:32.540 --> 00:16:33.950 align:middle line:84%
This one, you were in
my masters thesis.

00:16:33.950 --> 00:16:37.180 align:middle line:90%


00:16:37.180 --> 00:16:44.470 align:middle line:84%
Verisimilitude is basically a
tension when you're designing

00:16:44.470 --> 00:16:45.830 align:middle line:90%
a game mechanic.

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:48.200 align:middle line:84%
And that tension is telling
you, I want to make this

00:16:48.200 --> 00:16:52.930 align:middle line:84%
experience as immersive and as
true to life as possible.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:57.635 align:middle line:84%
And the opposite of that is
what I call dissociation.

00:16:57.635 --> 00:16:58.580 align:middle line:84%
Which is really hard
to pronounce.

00:16:58.580 --> 00:17:01.390 align:middle line:84%
It's not like I just
call it separation.

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:04.910 align:middle line:84%
But, how do you draw that line
between who I am and who my

00:17:04.910 --> 00:17:06.230 align:middle line:90%
character is?

00:17:06.230 --> 00:17:09.640 align:middle line:84%
And I'll give you a couple
of examples just to

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:10.890 align:middle line:90%
get inside this now.

00:17:10.890 --> 00:17:16.290 align:middle line:90%


00:17:16.290 --> 00:17:18.670 align:middle line:84%
Who doesn't know what
a live-action

00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:19.750 align:middle line:90%
role-playing game is?

00:17:19.750 --> 00:17:21.819 align:middle line:84%
Or doesn't have a
good clear idea?

00:17:21.819 --> 00:17:25.119 align:middle line:90%


00:17:25.119 --> 00:17:25.849 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:17:25.849 --> 00:17:29.856 align:middle line:84%
So maybe, a little bit of
background just so that I can

00:17:29.856 --> 00:17:31.106 align:middle line:90%
use the examples.

00:17:31.106 --> 00:17:32.950 align:middle line:90%


00:17:32.950 --> 00:17:36.310 align:middle line:84%
Do you folks know how a
table-top role-playing game

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:37.560 align:middle line:90%
generally works?

00:17:37.560 --> 00:17:39.880 align:middle line:90%


00:17:39.880 --> 00:17:40.150 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:17:40.150 --> 00:17:42.200 align:middle line:90%
So, start from scratch.

00:17:42.200 --> 00:17:45.450 align:middle line:90%
Imagine improv theatre.

00:17:45.450 --> 00:17:49.100 align:middle line:84%
Imagine you have an environment
where you have a

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:52.030 align:middle line:84%
bunch of people who
want to enact some

00:17:52.030 --> 00:17:55.620 align:middle line:90%
sort of scene together.

00:17:55.620 --> 00:17:58.010 align:middle line:84%
In theatre, a scene is usually
something like five minutes,

00:17:58.010 --> 00:18:00.020 align:middle line:90%
maybe half an hour.

00:18:00.020 --> 00:18:02.855 align:middle line:84%
In a live-action role-playing
game, that's more like an hour

00:18:02.855 --> 00:18:07.300 align:middle line:90%
to 10 days to 30 days long.

00:18:07.300 --> 00:18:10.150 align:middle line:90%
A much longer period of time.

00:18:10.150 --> 00:18:11.850 align:middle line:84%
But the motivation
is the same.

00:18:11.850 --> 00:18:16.440 align:middle line:84%
We're in this space to enact
some sort of shared reality,

00:18:16.440 --> 00:18:20.300 align:middle line:84%
shared creative reality,
together.

00:18:20.300 --> 00:18:22.900 align:middle line:84%
In a role-playing game
that's done by the

00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:25.560 align:middle line:90%
usual addition of rules.

00:18:25.560 --> 00:18:28.850 align:middle line:84%
And usually some sort of world
setting and some sort of

00:18:28.850 --> 00:18:30.370 align:middle line:90%
character description.

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:34.170 align:middle line:84%
And how these things are told
to you could be as simple as

00:18:34.170 --> 00:18:36.980 align:middle line:84%
the game master walks up to
each person and explains

00:18:36.980 --> 00:18:39.440 align:middle line:90%
what's going on.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:47.750 align:middle line:84%
You are all cats in an alley-way
and some of you are

00:18:47.750 --> 00:18:48.620 align:middle line:90%
hungry and looking for food.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.560 align:middle line:84%
Some of you are on top of the
pecking order and make sure

00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:53.610 align:middle line:90%
you get all of the food.

00:18:53.610 --> 00:18:54.750 align:middle line:90%
Here's how you eat food.

00:18:54.750 --> 00:18:56.390 align:middle line:84%
Here's how you move
food around.

00:18:56.390 --> 00:18:57.810 align:middle line:90%
Go.

00:18:57.810 --> 00:19:00.730 align:middle line:90%
That sort of thing.

00:19:00.730 --> 00:19:03.700 align:middle line:84%
A game master could just do it
at that level, and you've got

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:04.720 align:middle line:84%
a live-action role-playing
game.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:06.486 align:middle line:84%
There's usually some sort of end
condition or at least some

00:19:06.486 --> 00:19:08.738 align:middle line:84%
sort of time limit to tell
you when the game's over.

00:19:08.738 --> 00:19:11.390 align:middle line:90%


00:19:11.390 --> 00:19:14.400 align:middle line:84%
When you get a little bit more
detailed, and when you start

00:19:14.400 --> 00:19:17.235 align:middle line:84%
mixing and matching it with the
conventions of other kind

00:19:17.235 --> 00:19:19.560 align:middle line:84%
of role-playing games--
computer and table-top

00:19:19.560 --> 00:19:21.000 align:middle line:90%
role-playing games--

00:19:21.000 --> 00:19:24.310 align:middle line:84%
you have things like character
sheets where you're given your

00:19:24.310 --> 00:19:26.860 align:middle line:84%
information on a piece of paper,
or you create your

00:19:26.860 --> 00:19:29.650 align:middle line:84%
information on a
piece of paper.

00:19:29.650 --> 00:19:31.850 align:middle line:84%
And that information is
information that's only

00:19:31.850 --> 00:19:35.280 align:middle line:84%
available to you and
the game master.

00:19:35.280 --> 00:19:36.700 align:middle line:84%
A secret information for all
intents and purposes.

00:19:36.700 --> 00:19:40.790 align:middle line:90%


00:19:40.790 --> 00:19:43.640 align:middle line:84%
You will use different means
to convey chunks of the

00:19:43.640 --> 00:19:45.630 align:middle line:84%
information to the rest
of the world.

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:51.050 align:middle line:84%
Like I'm actually not
female, but I'm

00:19:51.050 --> 00:19:52.540 align:middle line:90%
playing a female character.

00:19:52.540 --> 00:19:54.930 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll wear a badge
or something that has

00:19:54.930 --> 00:19:56.290 align:middle line:90%
my gender on it.

00:19:56.290 --> 00:20:01.860 align:middle line:84%
And that tells the rest of the
world treat me like a female.

00:20:01.860 --> 00:20:04.290 align:middle line:90%
That's one example.

00:20:04.290 --> 00:20:08.510 align:middle line:84%
I may actually be psychic, but
the rest of the world can't

00:20:08.510 --> 00:20:09.560 align:middle line:90%
necessarily see that.

00:20:09.560 --> 00:20:11.300 align:middle line:84%
Except for maybe other
psychic people.

00:20:11.300 --> 00:20:14.500 align:middle line:84%
So maybe I'll have a little
Greek symbol or something--

00:20:14.500 --> 00:20:16.410 align:middle line:84%
not Psi, that would
be too obvious--

00:20:16.410 --> 00:20:20.380 align:middle line:90%


00:20:20.380 --> 00:20:23.820 align:middle line:84%
or maybe my badge is just a
different color or something.

00:20:23.820 --> 00:20:26.070 align:middle line:84%
And people who are not psychic
have no idea what that means.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:28.200 align:middle line:84%
And people who are playing other
psychic characters now

00:20:28.200 --> 00:20:30.200 align:middle line:84%
see, [INAUDIBLE] has
a red badge.

00:20:30.200 --> 00:20:31.450 align:middle line:90%
I am sensing psychically.

00:20:31.450 --> 00:20:35.300 align:middle line:90%


00:20:35.300 --> 00:20:37.960 align:middle line:84%
And then there are other game
mechanics that I can do with

00:20:37.960 --> 00:20:38.590 align:middle line:90%
that person.

00:20:38.590 --> 00:20:40.497 align:middle line:84%
Actually all the psychic people
know each other's cell

00:20:40.497 --> 00:20:43.290 align:middle line:84%
phone numbers or something and
can talk to each other.

00:20:43.290 --> 00:20:45.140 align:middle line:90%
That's an example.

00:20:45.140 --> 00:20:46.410 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's awesome.

00:20:46.410 --> 00:20:47.945 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That would be
to your advantage.

00:20:47.945 --> 00:20:53.830 align:middle line:90%


00:20:53.830 --> 00:20:59.914 align:middle line:84%
So the connection with improv
theatre is actually stronger

00:20:59.914 --> 00:21:01.610 align:middle line:84%
than a lot of people may
generally assume.

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:05.550 align:middle line:84%
Because a lot of people think
of live-action role-playing

00:21:05.550 --> 00:21:07.630 align:middle line:84%
games and they focus on
the games part of it.

00:21:07.630 --> 00:21:11.800 align:middle line:84%
And they think of it as a RPG
in the computer sense.

00:21:11.800 --> 00:21:16.300 align:middle line:84%
So your role is your character
in the sense of, I have my

00:21:16.300 --> 00:21:19.146 align:middle line:84%
character's stats, my
character's abilities, a lot

00:21:19.146 --> 00:21:21.860 align:middle line:84%
of these steps and abilities
don't necessarily need to be

00:21:21.860 --> 00:21:22.430 align:middle line:90%
written down.

00:21:22.430 --> 00:21:24.730 align:middle line:84%
Because, again, you're dealing
with a human body.

00:21:24.730 --> 00:21:28.114 align:middle line:84%
So, how strong is
my character?

00:21:28.114 --> 00:21:28.598 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

00:21:28.598 --> 00:21:30.730 align:middle line:90%
How strong are you?

00:21:30.730 --> 00:21:32.930 align:middle line:90%
How long a sword do I have?

00:21:32.930 --> 00:21:36.030 align:middle line:84%
Well, how long is that piece
of foam over there?

00:21:36.030 --> 00:21:39.195 align:middle line:90%
That's your sword now.

00:21:39.195 --> 00:21:40.445 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:21:40.445 --> 00:21:42.288 align:middle line:90%


00:21:42.288 --> 00:21:43.350 align:middle line:84%
I played a character that
was supposed to

00:21:43.350 --> 00:21:44.180 align:middle line:90%
be very, very, strong.

00:21:44.180 --> 00:21:45.490 align:middle line:84%
And I am not very,
very, strong.

00:21:45.490 --> 00:21:50.902 align:middle line:84%
But I had a disclaimer badge
and the that it manifested

00:21:50.902 --> 00:21:52.255 align:middle line:84%
itself in game play was that at
some point they challenged

00:21:52.255 --> 00:21:54.346 align:middle line:84%
someone to an arm-wrestling
duel.

00:21:54.346 --> 00:21:55.830 align:middle line:90%
And they made sure--

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:57.730 align:middle line:84%
they looked at my stat
and saw, oh I

00:21:57.730 --> 00:21:58.605 align:middle line:90%
know what this means.

00:21:58.605 --> 00:22:00.173 align:middle line:84%
And so even though they were
about twice my size they

00:22:00.173 --> 00:22:02.354 align:middle line:90%
pretended to lose.

00:22:02.354 --> 00:22:03.604 align:middle line:90%
It was very entertaining.

00:22:03.604 --> 00:22:06.130 align:middle line:90%


00:22:06.130 --> 00:22:08.450 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So the spectrum
of on one hand

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.720 align:middle line:90%
there are these games.

00:22:10.720 --> 00:22:15.840 align:middle line:84%
The type of games that Jeremy
describes allows you to play

00:22:15.840 --> 00:22:18.200 align:middle line:84%
things that are physically
very different from you.

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:19.820 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
some live-action

00:22:19.820 --> 00:22:20.520 align:middle line:90%
role-playing games--

00:22:20.520 --> 00:22:22.520 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Where it's just
how strong you are.

00:22:22.520 --> 00:22:24.110 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: --Yeah, it's just
how strong you are.

00:22:24.110 --> 00:22:26.260 align:middle line:84%
A lot of those are
like [INAUDIBLE]

00:22:26.260 --> 00:22:33.591 align:middle line:84%
LARPs, where people will dress
up in actual armor that

00:22:33.591 --> 00:22:34.210 align:middle line:84%
they've decided to make out
of leather or duct tape.

00:22:34.210 --> 00:22:38.620 align:middle line:84%
And they will swing around
swords instead of foam padded.

00:22:38.620 --> 00:22:41.010 align:middle line:84%
They'll go out to a
forest somewhere--

00:22:41.010 --> 00:22:43.950 align:middle line:90%
typically in New England--

00:22:43.950 --> 00:22:47.550 align:middle line:84%
and spend two days, three
days, in the middle of a

00:22:47.550 --> 00:22:51.490 align:middle line:84%
forest sleeping in a cabin
somewhere and pretending to

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:53.000 align:middle line:90%
fantasy adventure it.

00:22:53.000 --> 00:22:55.470 align:middle line:84%
And every once in awhile there
will be GM controlled

00:22:55.470 --> 00:22:57.170 align:middle line:84%
volunteers who will be wearing
[INAUDIBLE] masks.

00:22:57.170 --> 00:23:01.360 align:middle line:90%


00:23:01.360 --> 00:23:03.800 align:middle line:90%
That's on the other spectrum.

00:23:03.800 --> 00:23:05.330 align:middle line:90%
It's a spectrum.

00:23:05.330 --> 00:23:09.855 align:middle line:90%
It's not like the [INAUDIBLE]

00:23:09.855 --> 00:23:15.280 align:middle line:84%
LARPs where you run around with
sticks and are Assassins

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:20.626 align:middle line:84%
Guild type LARPs where most of
your abilities are governed by

00:23:20.626 --> 00:23:22.110 align:middle line:90%
numbers and stats.

00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:23.340 align:middle line:90%
Kind of this spectrum.

00:23:23.340 --> 00:23:26.880 align:middle line:84%
You can think of assassin guild
type thing as being very

00:23:26.880 --> 00:23:29.780 align:middle line:84%
much inspired by what you could
do on a computer or what

00:23:29.780 --> 00:23:31.860 align:middle line:84%
you could do on a
table-top LARP.

00:23:31.860 --> 00:23:34.190 align:middle line:84%
Because on a table-top LARP, how
strong your character is

00:23:34.190 --> 00:23:36.440 align:middle line:84%
has nothing to do with how
strong the player is.

00:23:36.440 --> 00:23:39.805 align:middle line:90%
It's what [INAUDIBLE].

00:23:39.805 --> 00:23:42.942 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Playing a game just
recently, I found out that in

00:23:42.942 --> 00:23:46.332 align:middle line:84%
New England there are basically
LARPs which get done

00:23:46.332 --> 00:23:49.710 align:middle line:90%
with rifles.

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:52.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: They're
military level.

00:23:52.560 --> 00:23:54.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But they're
not paintball.

00:23:54.620 --> 00:23:56.114 align:middle line:90%
So you shoot people.

00:23:56.114 --> 00:23:56.612 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: With plastic
pellets?

00:23:56.612 --> 00:23:59.110 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:01.410 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, air-soft is what
they call them.

00:24:01.410 --> 00:24:05.310 align:middle line:84%
And so one team will dress up as
US soldiers, and the other

00:24:05.310 --> 00:24:06.990 align:middle line:84%
ones are like middle-eastern
soldiers.

00:24:06.990 --> 00:24:10.548 align:middle line:90%


00:24:10.548 --> 00:24:12.010 align:middle line:84%
It's elaborate and slightly
distasteful.

00:24:12.010 --> 00:24:14.960 align:middle line:90%


00:24:14.960 --> 00:24:16.368 align:middle line:84%
You can go and look
at their website.

00:24:16.368 --> 00:24:18.997 align:middle line:84%
I think it's the New England
Airsoft Association or

00:24:18.997 --> 00:24:19.714 align:middle line:90%
something like that.

00:24:19.714 --> 00:24:24.810 align:middle line:84%
And they have excerpts of
a video cam of these.

00:24:24.810 --> 00:24:28.876 align:middle line:84%
And there's negotiation
between the two sides

00:24:28.876 --> 00:24:29.650 align:middle line:90%
and what have you.

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:33.610 align:middle line:84%
They actually take on the roles
of the insurgents or the

00:24:33.610 --> 00:24:35.095 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:24:35.095 --> 00:24:37.075 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes they're just
shooting each other.

00:24:37.075 --> 00:24:39.330 align:middle line:84%
Other times they're trying to
find out who in the town who

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:42.040 align:middle line:90%
is the bad guy.

00:24:42.040 --> 00:24:43.200 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Sometimes it
gets more elaborate.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:46.690 align:middle line:84%
And some of us are actually
going to be [INAUDIBLE] and

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:49.550 align:middle line:84%
some of us are actually going to
be combatants and you have

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:52.250 align:middle line:90%
to figure out who's who.

00:24:52.250 --> 00:24:53.710 align:middle line:84%
That one will be really,
really, interesting

00:24:53.710 --> 00:24:54.960 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE] for IEDs.

00:24:54.960 --> 00:24:58.180 align:middle line:90%


00:24:58.180 --> 00:25:02.610 align:middle line:84%
That's something that annoys
me about games that involve

00:25:02.610 --> 00:25:04.640 align:middle line:90%
Middle Eastern combatants.

00:25:04.640 --> 00:25:06.680 align:middle line:84%
Is that, they don't have
rules for IEDs.

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:10.260 align:middle line:84%
And that's the primary form of
contact in those states.

00:25:10.260 --> 00:25:14.720 align:middle line:90%


00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:16.770 align:middle line:90%
That's to the point.

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:20.910 align:middle line:84%
Military exercises a lot of them
are basically live-action

00:25:20.910 --> 00:25:22.360 align:middle line:90%
role-playing games.

00:25:22.360 --> 00:25:25.830 align:middle line:84%
All the way from I'm actually
running around with a gun full

00:25:25.830 --> 00:25:29.940 align:middle line:84%
of blanks or shooting pellets or
shooting a laser beam that

00:25:29.940 --> 00:25:33.910 align:middle line:84%
is going to register on some
other soldier's gear.

00:25:33.910 --> 00:25:35.666 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: We call
them war games.

00:25:35.666 --> 00:25:36.831 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: We call them--

00:25:36.831 --> 00:25:38.081 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:25:38.081 --> 00:25:40.268 align:middle line:90%


00:25:40.268 --> 00:25:41.518 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: --Yes, that's
a good thing.

00:25:41.518 --> 00:25:44.010 align:middle line:90%


00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:45.670 align:middle line:84%
Then you also have the other
end of military simulations

00:25:45.670 --> 00:25:48.400 align:middle line:84%
where it's just a bunch
of people in a room

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.190 align:middle line:90%
looking at a screen.

00:25:50.190 --> 00:25:54.350 align:middle line:84%
Or not even a screen, a white
board with a world map on it,

00:25:54.350 --> 00:25:59.140 align:middle line:84%
and they're moving forces around
like a table player.

00:25:59.140 --> 00:26:02.330 align:middle line:84%
But those are still considered
live-action action things,

00:26:02.330 --> 00:26:06.030 align:middle line:84%
because every person in the room
is representing someone

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:08.100 align:middle line:84%
in the chain of command that
would normally be in a war

00:26:08.100 --> 00:26:09.915 align:middle line:90%
room attack.

00:26:09.915 --> 00:26:11.895 align:middle line:90%
I am the commander.

00:26:11.895 --> 00:26:14.170 align:middle line:84%
You are the lieutenant, or
something like that, and I'm

00:26:14.170 --> 00:26:19.930 align:middle line:84%
going to say, get me the status
of Washington D.C. Has

00:26:19.930 --> 00:26:22.330 align:middle line:90%
it been nuked yet?

00:26:22.330 --> 00:26:25.871 align:middle line:84%
And the lieutenant says we have
three missiles coming in

00:26:25.871 --> 00:26:28.230 align:middle line:90%
based on some look up.

00:26:28.230 --> 00:26:31.030 align:middle line:84%
The term for that is typically
called creep [INAUDIBLE],

00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:35.820 align:middle line:84%
especially when it's
real people playing

00:26:35.820 --> 00:26:37.980 align:middle line:90%
against real people.

00:26:37.980 --> 00:26:41.910 align:middle line:84%
It's two or more player game
where not everyone is

00:26:41.910 --> 00:26:43.050 align:middle line:90%
one the same side.

00:26:43.050 --> 00:26:46.940 align:middle line:84%
If it's just on one side then
just the war game terminology

00:26:46.940 --> 00:26:49.790 align:middle line:90%
tends to be more a catch all.

00:26:49.790 --> 00:26:53.350 align:middle line:84%
But when you have two players on
each side, and are playing

00:26:53.350 --> 00:26:55.620 align:middle line:84%
through these things,
how results get

00:26:55.620 --> 00:27:00.470 align:middle line:90%
resolved also can vary.

00:27:00.470 --> 00:27:03.090 align:middle line:90%
There are obviously digital and

00:27:03.090 --> 00:27:04.980 align:middle line:90%
mathematical ways to compute.

00:27:04.980 --> 00:27:12.930 align:middle line:84%
OK, I've given an order to fire
a missile at Singapore.

00:27:12.930 --> 00:27:17.620 align:middle line:84%
And it's going to take two
hours for it to land.

00:27:17.620 --> 00:27:20.310 align:middle line:84%
And there's a percentage
that it might land.

00:27:20.310 --> 00:27:20.900 align:middle line:90%
You could do it.

00:27:20.900 --> 00:27:22.280 align:middle line:90%
You could do that way.

00:27:22.280 --> 00:27:25.670 align:middle line:84%
But traditionally, what's
actually been done is that you

00:27:25.670 --> 00:27:27.750 align:middle line:84%
say this is what I'm
going to do.

00:27:27.750 --> 00:27:30.325 align:middle line:84%
And someone far more experienced
than you, usually

00:27:30.325 --> 00:27:33.760 align:middle line:84%
your instructor or one of
several instructors, takes

00:27:33.760 --> 00:27:38.320 align:middle line:84%
that information, goes into
another room, and talks to the

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:41.390 align:middle line:84%
people who are actually doing
the same thing with your

00:27:41.390 --> 00:27:42.660 align:middle line:90%
opposing team.

00:27:42.660 --> 00:27:45.540 align:middle line:84%
And they're just saying,
OK in this event

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:46.630 align:middle line:90%
what's going to happen?

00:27:46.630 --> 00:27:50.090 align:middle line:84%
And then they go back to
each team and they

00:27:50.090 --> 00:27:53.260 align:middle line:90%
report, missile missed.

00:27:53.260 --> 00:27:54.600 align:middle line:90%
Or, your troops engaged.

00:27:54.600 --> 00:27:58.980 align:middle line:84%
You found your opponent's troops
at this location and

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:00.850 align:middle line:84%
you've lost 50% of
your troops.

00:28:00.850 --> 00:28:01.180 align:middle line:90%
That sort of thing.

00:28:01.180 --> 00:28:04.860 align:middle line:84%
And that's the traditional
[INAUDIBLE].

00:28:04.860 --> 00:28:05.980 align:middle line:84%
The nice thing about that is
that takes advantage of the

00:28:05.980 --> 00:28:09.280 align:middle line:84%
fact that not just the players
but everybody is a live-action

00:28:09.280 --> 00:28:10.550 align:middle line:90%
human being.

00:28:10.550 --> 00:28:12.610 align:middle line:90%
Because you have flexibility.

00:28:12.610 --> 00:28:17.280 align:middle line:84%
You can do ridiculous things
that programmers or game

00:28:17.280 --> 00:28:21.820 align:middle line:84%
designers may not necessarily
have assumed were possible,

00:28:21.820 --> 00:28:24.830 align:middle line:84%
but happen to make sense
in the heat of

00:28:24.830 --> 00:28:26.170 align:middle line:90%
that particular game.

00:28:26.170 --> 00:28:28.420 align:middle line:84%
And you have real people
deciding, this is

00:28:28.420 --> 00:28:29.350 align:middle line:90%
what's going to happen.

00:28:29.350 --> 00:28:33.760 align:middle line:84%
So we'll make up a
logical solution.

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:35.780 align:middle line:84%
In the military, that's why you
want people who are really

00:28:35.780 --> 00:28:37.590 align:middle line:90%
experienced.

00:28:37.590 --> 00:28:40.595 align:middle line:84%
In a non-military live-action
role-playing game, like the

00:28:40.595 --> 00:28:45.400 align:middle line:84%
kind that's played around MIT,
people will still do that.

00:28:45.400 --> 00:28:51.000 align:middle line:84%
So I'm in a game where I have
a steampunk setting.

00:28:51.000 --> 00:28:56.620 align:middle line:84%
And I don't have all the
equipment I need to build this

00:28:56.620 --> 00:28:59.800 align:middle line:84%
machine of death that I'm
supposed to be completing.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:03.860 align:middle line:84%
But I could take apart some
of the other machines

00:29:03.860 --> 00:29:05.130 align:middle line:90%
that are in the world.

00:29:05.130 --> 00:29:07.050 align:middle line:84%
And then the game master
says, wait a minute.

00:29:07.050 --> 00:29:08.510 align:middle line:84%
We've got to put rules
about that.

00:29:08.510 --> 00:29:10.480 align:middle line:84%
Or, we don't want to start
anyone taking apart

00:29:10.480 --> 00:29:11.130 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:29:11.130 --> 00:29:13.780 align:middle line:84%
But it makes perfect sense
in this world.

00:29:13.780 --> 00:29:17.100 align:middle line:84%
So, OK, it's going to take you
two hours and you have to be

00:29:17.100 --> 00:29:17.390 align:middle line:90%
in this room.

00:29:17.390 --> 00:29:19.880 align:middle line:84%
But after that, you'll have
the parts you need.

00:29:19.880 --> 00:29:22.380 align:middle line:90%


00:29:22.380 --> 00:29:24.422 align:middle line:84%
The other side of live-action
role-playing is that often the

00:29:24.422 --> 00:29:26.470 align:middle line:84%
people who are controlling the
game of live-action say that

00:29:26.470 --> 00:29:28.360 align:middle line:90%
wasn't fair.

00:29:28.360 --> 00:29:33.720 align:middle line:84%
And again, they can improvise
along with the players.

00:29:33.720 --> 00:29:37.210 align:middle line:84%
So you have that bit
of flexibility.

00:29:37.210 --> 00:29:40.580 align:middle line:84%
You're limited by how
experienced most people are.

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:44.260 align:middle line:84%
People who don't tend to be
terribly experienced tend to

00:29:44.260 --> 00:29:49.154 align:middle line:84%
make rash decisions that
end up hurting the

00:29:49.154 --> 00:29:50.580 align:middle line:90%
playability of the game.

00:29:50.580 --> 00:29:51.450 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes they get lucky.

00:29:51.450 --> 00:29:53.640 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes they don't.

00:29:53.640 --> 00:30:01.462 align:middle line:84%
Let me see, one example early
on when I was making

00:30:01.462 --> 00:30:05.920 align:middle line:84%
live-action role-playing
games, was I had a game

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:12.240 align:middle line:84%
mechanic where if one of the
police officers in this sort

00:30:12.240 --> 00:30:17.730 align:middle line:84%
of detective scenario wanted to
do a background check they

00:30:17.730 --> 00:30:19.840 align:middle line:90%
could ask the game master.

00:30:19.840 --> 00:30:21.380 align:middle line:90%
Some amount of time--

00:30:21.380 --> 00:30:21.990 align:middle line:84%
actually it was fairly
instant.

00:30:21.990 --> 00:30:24.660 align:middle line:84%
Because the very molded
kind of system.

00:30:24.660 --> 00:30:26.880 align:middle line:84%
The assumption was that they
will find a computer and

00:30:26.880 --> 00:30:28.070 align:middle line:90%
they'll look it up.

00:30:28.070 --> 00:30:30.370 align:middle line:84%
And we'll give them
information.

00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:32.740 align:middle line:84%
And that led to two problems
was that, first of all what

00:30:32.740 --> 00:30:35.740 align:middle line:84%
happens if I'm not physically
in the same location as the

00:30:35.740 --> 00:30:37.050 align:middle line:84%
person who wants the
information?

00:30:37.050 --> 00:30:38.980 align:middle line:84%
And this is weird to think
of a computer with

00:30:38.980 --> 00:30:40.538 align:middle line:90%
legs walking around.

00:30:40.538 --> 00:30:43.870 align:middle line:84%
It sounds very science
fiction.

00:30:43.870 --> 00:30:47.200 align:middle line:84%
And on the other hand, it means
that I needed to have

00:30:47.200 --> 00:30:50.266 align:middle line:84%
all the information about
everybody at my

00:30:50.266 --> 00:30:53.080 align:middle line:90%
finger tips at all times.

00:30:53.080 --> 00:30:55.110 align:middle line:90%
Which often wasn't true.

00:30:55.110 --> 00:30:57.500 align:middle line:84%
So someone would come in, I
need the information about

00:30:57.500 --> 00:30:58.670 align:middle line:90%
this character.

00:30:58.670 --> 00:31:00.090 align:middle line:90%
I'm performing a look up.

00:31:00.090 --> 00:31:03.488 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, I'll get back
to you in two hours.

00:31:03.488 --> 00:31:07.057 align:middle line:84%
Because I don't have the
information here with me I'm

00:31:07.057 --> 00:31:09.470 align:middle line:84%
going to look it up and I'll
give it to you later.

00:31:09.470 --> 00:31:11.456 align:middle line:84%
Now I don't make that mistake
anymore, but that was the kind

00:31:11.456 --> 00:31:15.330 align:middle line:84%
of mistake I made when
I was a [INAUDIBLE].

00:31:15.330 --> 00:31:18.440 align:middle line:84%
Because I hadn't really thought
about what that would

00:31:18.440 --> 00:31:20.720 align:middle line:84%
require from me as
a game master.

00:31:20.720 --> 00:31:24.510 align:middle line:84%
Now that's a different
tension.

00:31:24.510 --> 00:31:26.310 align:middle line:84%
I am actually going to read
a chunk of this--

00:31:26.310 --> 00:31:31.570 align:middle line:90%


00:31:31.570 --> 00:31:32.820 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:31:32.820 --> 00:31:35.450 align:middle line:90%


00:31:35.450 --> 00:31:38.360 align:middle line:84%
I talked about dissociation
or separation.

00:31:38.360 --> 00:31:43.700 align:middle line:90%


00:31:43.700 --> 00:31:47.150 align:middle line:84%
And what I'm talking about with
that mistake that I made

00:31:47.150 --> 00:31:50.000 align:middle line:90%
is just plain old feasibility.

00:31:50.000 --> 00:31:53.415 align:middle line:84%
Something that you probably have
already run into it with

00:31:53.415 --> 00:31:54.390 align:middle line:84%
the games that you've
designed.

00:31:54.390 --> 00:31:55.640 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:31:55.640 --> 00:31:59.270 align:middle line:90%


00:31:59.270 --> 00:32:00.880 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And many of the games
that you've designed

00:32:00.880 --> 00:32:03.440 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] could have thought
of as just plane of scope.

00:32:03.440 --> 00:32:06.410 align:middle line:84%
It's like, how big
is this game?

00:32:06.410 --> 00:32:08.550 align:middle line:84%
How long is it going
to take us to put

00:32:08.550 --> 00:32:10.962 align:middle line:90%
all these bits together?

00:32:10.962 --> 00:32:12.212 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:32:12.212 --> 00:32:16.490 align:middle line:90%


00:32:16.490 --> 00:32:18.480 align:middle line:84%
Can we order these
bits in time?

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:20.130 align:middle line:84%
And are they going to
be delivered in

00:32:20.130 --> 00:32:21.380 align:middle line:90%
time for the class?

00:32:21.380 --> 00:32:25.130 align:middle line:84%
I know multiple teams
had this problem.

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:26.380 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:32:26.380 --> 00:32:33.850 align:middle line:90%


00:32:33.850 --> 00:32:35.030 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Let me see if
I can combine some

00:32:35.030 --> 00:32:38.430 align:middle line:90%
of these ideas together.

00:32:38.430 --> 00:32:43.200 align:middle line:84%
One thing that the Assassin
Guild likes to have are people

00:32:43.200 --> 00:32:46.010 align:middle line:90%
who have locked memories.

00:32:46.010 --> 00:32:50.830 align:middle line:84%
They know something, but they
don't know that they know it.

00:32:50.830 --> 00:32:52.990 align:middle line:84%
Little memory packets, little
pieces of paper that they

00:32:52.990 --> 00:32:54.850 align:middle line:84%
printed out and folded
up and stapled.

00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:57.435 align:middle line:90%


00:32:57.435 --> 00:33:02.980 align:middle line:84%
Takes you about 20 seconds
to make one of those.

00:33:02.980 --> 00:33:06.090 align:middle line:84%
Now imagine a game that
has maybe 60 people.

00:33:06.090 --> 00:33:11.020 align:middle line:84%
And each person maybe has five
locked memories because the

00:33:11.020 --> 00:33:13.670 align:middle line:90%
GM's really love the idea.

00:33:13.670 --> 00:33:17.200 align:middle line:90%
And 5 times 3 is

00:33:17.200 --> 00:33:19.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's only
about an hour.

00:33:19.100 --> 00:33:20.070 align:middle line:90%
Only about an hour.

00:33:20.070 --> 00:33:20.860 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Only about an hour.

00:33:20.860 --> 00:33:23.530 align:middle line:84%
Does it ever only actually
take an hour to actually

00:33:23.530 --> 00:33:24.330 align:middle line:90%
produce that?

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:31.860 align:middle line:84%
You get staple cuts and you
have to write them all.

00:33:31.860 --> 00:33:34.652 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It's a little less
than two hours actually.

00:33:34.652 --> 00:33:35.740 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: No way.

00:33:35.740 --> 00:33:38.454 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: 20 seconds
per 60 people.

00:33:38.454 --> 00:33:41.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But it's not
20 seconds each.

00:33:41.260 --> 00:33:43.110 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And then what happens
is that oh, wow, we

00:33:43.110 --> 00:33:47.610 align:middle line:84%
have 300 pieces of paper all
of which are now folded and

00:33:47.610 --> 00:33:49.470 align:middle line:84%
almost indistinguishable
from each other.

00:33:49.470 --> 00:33:51.138 align:middle line:84%
And we've got to figure
out who gets which.

00:33:51.138 --> 00:33:53.770 align:middle line:90%


00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:57.910 align:middle line:90%
And these are the concerns.

00:33:57.910 --> 00:34:00.850 align:middle line:90%
That's a feasibility issue.

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:04.660 align:middle line:84%
Doing something like that for
one or two players is

00:34:04.660 --> 00:34:05.790 align:middle line:90%
extremely feasible.

00:34:05.790 --> 00:34:09.780 align:middle line:84%
It takes almost no time
for the game master.

00:34:09.780 --> 00:34:12.820 align:middle line:84%
Doing that for your entire
player base, if you have a 60

00:34:12.820 --> 00:34:17.239 align:middle line:84%
person game, on the large scale
becomes the majority of

00:34:17.239 --> 00:34:19.280 align:middle line:90%
your prep time.

00:34:19.280 --> 00:34:21.880 align:middle line:84%
Suddenly you're setting
aside a whole day just

00:34:21.880 --> 00:34:22.679 align:middle line:90%
to deal with that.

00:34:22.679 --> 00:34:26.272 align:middle line:84%
And a game's only three days
long or something like that.

00:34:26.272 --> 00:34:27.522 align:middle line:90%
With that level of effort.

00:34:27.522 --> 00:34:29.639 align:middle line:90%


00:34:29.639 --> 00:34:32.420 align:middle line:84%
Things to preserve the
game master's sanity.

00:34:32.420 --> 00:34:36.670 align:middle line:90%


00:34:36.670 --> 00:34:42.070 align:middle line:84%
The MIT janitorial staff,
facility staff, are pretty

00:34:42.070 --> 00:34:45.090 align:middle line:84%
ruthless when it comes to
picking up stuff that has

00:34:45.090 --> 00:34:46.320 align:middle line:90%
fallen on the floor.

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:49.480 align:middle line:84%
So say that's a really important
piece of paper that

00:34:49.480 --> 00:34:53.060 align:middle line:84%
you've taped up on the wall
somewhere's here at MIT.

00:34:53.060 --> 00:34:56.120 align:middle line:84%
And there's information that
this is the place that your

00:34:56.120 --> 00:34:57.390 align:middle line:90%
players are looking for.

00:34:57.390 --> 00:34:59.410 align:middle line:84%
They've combed all of
campus and they need

00:34:59.410 --> 00:35:00.750 align:middle line:90%
to find this location.

00:35:00.750 --> 00:35:03.622 align:middle line:84%
And that tape that you
used just wasn't that

00:35:03.622 --> 00:35:05.450 align:middle line:90%
strong and it fell.

00:35:05.450 --> 00:35:07.530 align:middle line:84%
And the janitorial staff
just picks it up

00:35:07.530 --> 00:35:08.830 align:middle line:90%
and throws it away.

00:35:08.830 --> 00:35:10.560 align:middle line:90%
Now what happens to your game?

00:35:10.560 --> 00:35:14.210 align:middle line:90%
Can your game even end?

00:35:14.210 --> 00:35:16.360 align:middle line:84%
Are you just going to have a
whole bunch of really tired

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:22.010 align:middle line:84%
players chasing you down
as the game master?

00:35:22.010 --> 00:35:27.260 align:middle line:84%
So the way that you resolve
this can be built into the

00:35:27.260 --> 00:35:28.510 align:middle line:90%
game mechanics.

00:35:28.510 --> 00:35:30.315 align:middle line:84%
You can build things like
multiple redundancy.

00:35:30.315 --> 00:35:35.120 align:middle line:84%
Like in addition to a sign there
will usually be this

00:35:35.120 --> 00:35:37.460 align:middle line:90%
other thing in this same area.

00:35:37.460 --> 00:35:39.730 align:middle line:84%
If you find one and you don't
find the other, it means

00:35:39.730 --> 00:35:41.910 align:middle line:90%
something has dropped off.

00:35:41.910 --> 00:35:43.910 align:middle line:84%
Involve the game master and the
game master will go and

00:35:43.910 --> 00:35:45.448 align:middle line:84%
fix it at some point in
time in the future.

00:35:45.448 --> 00:35:48.680 align:middle line:90%


00:35:48.680 --> 00:35:52.131 align:middle line:84%
If you have a game that has no
secret locations, and you have

00:35:52.131 --> 00:35:55.172 align:middle line:84%
a game that's campus wide, you
could just give people a map

00:35:55.172 --> 00:35:57.280 align:middle line:90%
of all the information.

00:35:57.280 --> 00:36:00.430 align:middle line:84%
Sure, you still want stuff on
the wall because it's neat to

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:03.080 align:middle line:84%
be able to walk into a classroom
like this and say,

00:36:03.080 --> 00:36:04.350 align:middle line:90%
oh mortuary.

00:36:04.350 --> 00:36:05.860 align:middle line:90%
That's where I am.

00:36:05.860 --> 00:36:10.004 align:middle line:84%
And then suddenly reminded, oh
maybe I should be role-playing

00:36:10.004 --> 00:36:12.890 align:middle line:84%
back then and that
sort of thing.

00:36:12.890 --> 00:36:17.800 align:middle line:84%
But it's sort of like walking
through a space and you have a

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:19.510 align:middle line:84%
map that told you you're
in a mortuary.

00:36:19.510 --> 00:36:20.740 align:middle line:84%
But you weren't looking at your
space, so you just walked

00:36:20.740 --> 00:36:23.270 align:middle line:84%
through the space in entry
to the classroom.

00:36:23.270 --> 00:36:27.010 align:middle line:84%
Yes, I am going to do my
tutoring in this room.

00:36:27.010 --> 00:36:30.790 align:middle line:84%
It's a morgue, why are
you doing tutoring?

00:36:30.790 --> 00:36:33.830 align:middle line:84%
So it's neat to be able to
have those things to give

00:36:33.830 --> 00:36:36.730 align:middle line:84%
players information about what's
going on in the space.

00:36:36.730 --> 00:36:40.630 align:middle line:84%
And to help them make believe,
help them imagine what they

00:36:40.630 --> 00:36:41.630 align:middle line:84%
could possibly do
in the space.

00:36:41.630 --> 00:36:43.530 align:middle line:84%
Maybe even give them clues about
what they need to be

00:36:43.530 --> 00:36:45.710 align:middle line:84%
doing in order to advance
their goals.

00:36:45.710 --> 00:36:50.450 align:middle line:84%
But then you also want to be
able to have backup plans for

00:36:50.450 --> 00:36:51.860 align:middle line:90%
when those things fail.

00:36:51.860 --> 00:36:53.860 align:middle line:90%
That's part of feasibility.

00:36:53.860 --> 00:36:56.310 align:middle line:84%
You don't want the game to break
halfway just because

00:36:56.310 --> 00:37:01.280 align:middle line:84%
janitorial staff came through or
the wind blew or something

00:37:01.280 --> 00:37:02.744 align:middle line:90%
and now your game sucks.

00:37:02.744 --> 00:37:06.960 align:middle line:90%


00:37:06.960 --> 00:37:08.210 align:middle line:84%
That's another type
of information.

00:37:08.210 --> 00:37:12.340 align:middle line:90%


00:37:12.340 --> 00:37:15.730 align:middle line:84%
And that's not entirely unlike
the information that we've

00:37:15.730 --> 00:37:20.190 align:middle line:84%
discussed in this class back
earlier this semester.

00:37:20.190 --> 00:37:26.370 align:middle line:84%
But with live-action games,
especially live-action games

00:37:26.370 --> 00:37:28.435 align:middle line:84%
that don't have any kind of
turn based thing, they're

00:37:28.435 --> 00:37:32.790 align:middle line:84%
happening in real time and
they're moving in real-time.

00:37:32.790 --> 00:37:36.880 align:middle line:84%
Information overload on the
player becomes something that

00:37:36.880 --> 00:37:39.007 align:middle line:84%
you have to be very careful of
when you're designing your

00:37:39.007 --> 00:37:39.510 align:middle line:90%
game mechanic.

00:37:39.510 --> 00:37:44.390 align:middle line:84%
How much is someone expected
to know at any given time?

00:37:44.390 --> 00:37:46.380 align:middle line:84%
This is going to be very
applicable for any of the

00:37:46.380 --> 00:37:49.370 align:middle line:84%
games that you design for
the third assignment.

00:37:49.370 --> 00:37:52.760 align:middle line:84%
If you decide to do a
live-action game, think about

00:37:52.760 --> 00:37:54.720 align:middle line:84%
here's somebody who's moving
around in a space.

00:37:54.720 --> 00:37:59.010 align:middle line:84%
Maybe it's a hospital or it may
be the doctor's office.

00:37:59.010 --> 00:38:05.010 align:middle line:84%
And this person is trying
to remember X numbers of

00:38:05.010 --> 00:38:07.880 align:middle line:84%
statistics about their
character.

00:38:07.880 --> 00:38:15.550 align:middle line:84%
How agitated I am, the number
that determines how many drugs

00:38:15.550 --> 00:38:21.090 align:middle line:84%
are part of me right now,
and that sort of thing.

00:38:21.090 --> 00:38:22.630 align:middle line:90%
You've got these stats.

00:38:22.630 --> 00:38:26.330 align:middle line:84%
And once again, too high, then
what happens is you've got a

00:38:26.330 --> 00:38:28.640 align:middle line:84%
character that's standing there
trying to do all the

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:31.630 align:middle line:84%
math in their head and
not role-playing.

00:38:31.630 --> 00:38:33.270 align:middle line:84%
If they're role-playing
anything, they're role-playing

00:38:33.270 --> 00:38:36.160 align:middle line:84%
a mathematician or a [INAUDIBLE]
in their minds.

00:38:36.160 --> 00:38:40.435 align:middle line:84%
And you're losing the immersion,
you're losing the

00:38:40.435 --> 00:38:43.300 align:middle line:84%
[INAUDIBLE] to be I'm supposed
to be an angry person.

00:38:43.300 --> 00:38:44.910 align:middle line:84%
But I'm standing here
trying to do math

00:38:44.910 --> 00:38:48.310 align:middle line:90%
about how angry I am.

00:38:48.310 --> 00:38:51.720 align:middle line:84%
And you've got attention
of information and very

00:38:51.720 --> 00:38:52.970 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:38:52.970 --> 00:38:54.470 align:middle line:90%


00:38:54.470 --> 00:38:57.940 align:middle line:84%
There are times when you
actually want that though.

00:38:57.940 --> 00:39:00.350 align:middle line:84%
There are ways to take
that and turn

00:39:00.350 --> 00:39:01.600 align:middle line:90%
it into your advantage.

00:39:01.600 --> 00:39:05.720 align:middle line:90%


00:39:05.720 --> 00:39:08.290 align:middle line:84%
In particular, when the
thing that you want

00:39:08.290 --> 00:39:09.550 align:middle line:90%
people to be doing--

00:39:09.550 --> 00:39:12.930 align:middle line:84%
the kind of activity that you
want people to engage in--

00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:14.940 align:middle line:84%
are the things that players
would generally be

00:39:14.940 --> 00:39:16.350 align:middle line:84%
uncomfortable doing
with each other.

00:39:16.350 --> 00:39:19.360 align:middle line:84%
Physical violence would be
a good example of that.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:23.800 align:middle line:84%
I want to be able to hurt this
other character, but I don't

00:39:23.800 --> 00:39:25.950 align:middle line:84%
want to hurt this
other player.

00:39:25.950 --> 00:39:30.120 align:middle line:84%
So what are the things that I
can do to bridge that gap?

00:39:30.120 --> 00:39:32.310 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Insult his mother.

00:39:32.310 --> 00:39:35.170 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Verbally, sure, there
are ways to do that.

00:39:35.170 --> 00:39:39.160 align:middle line:84%
But you might need rules or at
least some guidance from the

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:42.280 align:middle line:84%
GM to say, take that
seriously.

00:39:42.280 --> 00:39:46.040 align:middle line:84%
Because that can very easily
drift into the realm of, yeah

00:39:46.040 --> 00:39:47.290 align:middle line:90%
that's just a joke.

00:39:47.290 --> 00:39:49.490 align:middle line:90%


00:39:49.490 --> 00:39:50.630 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Rock, paper,
scissors?

00:39:50.630 --> 00:39:52.550 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Rock, paper,
scissors?

00:39:52.550 --> 00:39:56.670 align:middle line:84%
That could be a mechanic for
a fist fight, for instance.

00:39:56.670 --> 00:39:57.990 align:middle line:90%
Or [INAUDIBLE]

00:39:57.990 --> 00:40:00.070 align:middle line:84%
that you start see them
avoiding that.

00:40:00.070 --> 00:40:01.890 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Nerf guns.

00:40:01.890 --> 00:40:02.650 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Nerf guns?

00:40:02.650 --> 00:40:04.965 align:middle line:84%
Things that shoot little
pieces of plastic.

00:40:04.965 --> 00:40:08.640 align:middle line:90%


00:40:08.640 --> 00:40:11.015 align:middle line:90%
But odds and evens.

00:40:11.015 --> 00:40:12.512 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How about tokens
like in [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:12.512 --> 00:40:13.380 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Say what?

00:40:13.380 --> 00:40:16.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Health tokens
like in [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:16.680 --> 00:40:17.930 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Give me
some more detail.

00:40:17.930 --> 00:40:20.260 align:middle line:90%


00:40:20.260 --> 00:40:23.300 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Like if someone was a
certain number of flags, and

00:40:23.300 --> 00:40:25.160 align:middle line:84%
so you hurt them
by taking one.

00:40:25.160 --> 00:40:26.260 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So the player
might have

00:40:26.260 --> 00:40:27.855 align:middle line:90%
multiple flags for instance.

00:40:27.855 --> 00:40:29.900 align:middle line:90%
OK, gets them angry.

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:31.150 align:middle line:84%
It's like health bar
almost, actually.

00:40:31.150 --> 00:40:36.620 align:middle line:90%


00:40:36.620 --> 00:40:37.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:40:37.870 --> 00:40:43.370 align:middle line:90%


00:40:43.370 --> 00:40:46.470 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So dice rolling is
under the general term like

00:40:46.470 --> 00:40:49.530 align:middle line:90%
things randomizing numbers.

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:52.750 align:middle line:84%
In fact, rock, paper, scissors,
is randomizing

00:40:52.750 --> 00:40:53.340 align:middle line:90%
things almost.

00:40:53.340 --> 00:40:57.040 align:middle line:84%
And then you have toy
weapons, right?

00:40:57.040 --> 00:41:01.120 align:middle line:84%
Probably not so relevant
for the game violence.

00:41:01.120 --> 00:41:05.330 align:middle line:84%
But you could imagine setting up
a room full of foam things

00:41:05.330 --> 00:41:09.240 align:middle line:84%
which you will be quite happy
to throw at each other.

00:41:09.240 --> 00:41:13.620 align:middle line:84%
You know that this cylinder of
foam, of pipe-insulating

00:41:13.620 --> 00:41:17.260 align:middle line:84%
material, is not going to hurt
anyone when you throw it.

00:41:17.260 --> 00:41:20.000 align:middle line:84%
Which makes it more likely that
I'm going to pick it up

00:41:20.000 --> 00:41:21.950 align:middle line:90%
and throw it at someone.

00:41:21.950 --> 00:41:24.835 align:middle line:84%
Clearly it's something that's
designed for me to throw that

00:41:24.835 --> 00:41:26.670 align:middle line:90%
the GMs put there.

00:41:26.670 --> 00:41:29.560 align:middle line:84%
And if you want your players to
be engaging in that kind of

00:41:29.560 --> 00:41:31.700 align:middle line:90%
activity that's technically--

00:41:31.700 --> 00:41:33.090 align:middle line:84%
you could have put a
real bottle there.

00:41:33.090 --> 00:41:37.640 align:middle line:84%
You could have put a real mug
or computer or something.

00:41:37.640 --> 00:41:39.540 align:middle line:84%
That's the reason why you as
the GM could have, but they

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:43.610 align:middle line:84%
are deliberately designing to
put something fake there to

00:41:43.610 --> 00:41:47.040 align:middle line:84%
clue the player into maybe
there's something I can do

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:49.220 align:middle line:84%
with that I wouldn't necessarily
want to do if it

00:41:49.220 --> 00:41:50.774 align:middle line:90%
was the real thing.

00:41:50.774 --> 00:41:53.264 align:middle line:84%
I wouldn't want to throw a real
bottle at somebody, but I

00:41:53.264 --> 00:41:56.760 align:middle line:84%
would easily throw a foam
bottle at somebody.

00:41:56.760 --> 00:42:03.600 align:middle line:84%
Same thing for Nerf guns, dart
guns, this guns, it's like you

00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:06.320 align:middle line:84%
put something with a trigger
in somebody's hands that's

00:42:06.320 --> 00:42:09.670 align:middle line:84%
going to launch a little cheap
piece of plastic at someone?

00:42:09.670 --> 00:42:12.160 align:middle line:84%
They're itching for a chance
to use it, really.

00:42:12.160 --> 00:42:15.740 align:middle line:90%


00:42:15.740 --> 00:42:19.840 align:middle line:90%
Let's see, what else?

00:42:19.840 --> 00:42:21.060 align:middle line:90%
Dice are difficult.

00:42:21.060 --> 00:42:23.240 align:middle line:84%
The problem with dice is you
need a flat surface to roll

00:42:23.240 --> 00:42:26.304 align:middle line:84%
them on, and you might
not have that.

00:42:26.304 --> 00:42:27.554 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:27.554 --> 00:42:31.520 align:middle line:90%


00:42:31.520 --> 00:42:34.350 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Still a little bit
risky, because they could end

00:42:34.350 --> 00:42:35.230 align:middle line:90%
up going all over the floor.

00:42:35.230 --> 00:42:37.725 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Another thing that
I've seen people do is some

00:42:37.725 --> 00:42:40.220 align:middle line:84%
kind of [INAUDIBLE]
style thing.

00:42:40.220 --> 00:42:44.461 align:middle line:84%
Where essentially they have
[INAUDIBLE] or something, you

00:42:44.461 --> 00:42:45.709 align:middle line:90%
might have created them.

00:42:45.709 --> 00:42:48.810 align:middle line:84%
You can beat a challenge by
giving one to the other player

00:42:48.810 --> 00:42:51.021 align:middle line:90%
that you're fighting with.

00:42:51.021 --> 00:42:52.271 align:middle line:90%
And then [INAUDIBLE].

00:42:52.271 --> 00:42:55.310 align:middle line:90%


00:42:55.310 --> 00:42:56.898 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So it's like the
amount of energy you've got.

00:42:56.898 --> 00:42:58.850 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, it's like the
amount of energy you've got.

00:42:58.850 --> 00:43:03.040 align:middle line:84%
And also, if you discard them or
give them to another player

00:43:03.040 --> 00:43:07.960 align:middle line:84%
then it's the difference between
these things are just

00:43:07.960 --> 00:43:12.236 align:middle line:84%
gone and they circulate
around.

00:43:12.236 --> 00:43:12.728 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:12.728 --> 00:43:16.418 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, so if there's
the circulation thing than you

00:43:16.418 --> 00:43:18.140 align:middle line:90%
can get whooped a few times.

00:43:18.140 --> 00:43:19.616 align:middle line:84%
But after being whooped then
suddenly you get a chance to

00:43:19.616 --> 00:43:21.092 align:middle line:90%
whoop back.

00:43:21.092 --> 00:43:25.028 align:middle line:84%
And then it becomes
less [INAUDIBLE].

00:43:25.028 --> 00:43:26.012 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That was
interesting.

00:43:26.012 --> 00:43:29.456 align:middle line:84%
This is the good part that I
want to talk about which is

00:43:29.456 --> 00:43:31.460 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:43:31.460 --> 00:43:34.570 align:middle line:84%
A topic that you guys are
already very familiar with.

00:43:34.570 --> 00:43:39.580 align:middle line:84%
But, at what point do your
game mechanics--

00:43:39.580 --> 00:43:42.270 align:middle line:84%
are you designing your game
mechanics not necessarily to

00:43:42.270 --> 00:43:46.260 align:middle line:84%
enhance the realism or to
encourage certain behaviors or

00:43:46.260 --> 00:43:48.790 align:middle line:84%
to give the player enough
information about what they're

00:43:48.790 --> 00:43:51.110 align:middle line:84%
allowed to do or what they
should be doing?

00:43:51.110 --> 00:43:53.880 align:middle line:84%
And you're just going into the
realm of how do I make this

00:43:53.880 --> 00:43:56.410 align:middle line:90%
competitively interesting?

00:43:56.410 --> 00:43:59.080 align:middle line:84%
Real fights, if you get
a bunch of blows,

00:43:59.080 --> 00:44:00.330 align:middle line:90%
you do not get stronger.

00:44:00.330 --> 00:44:03.190 align:middle line:90%


00:44:03.190 --> 00:44:07.825 align:middle line:84%
Well, if you're Bruce
[? Bannon ?] maybe.

00:44:07.825 --> 00:44:14.510 align:middle line:84%
But from a competitive point of
view, or from a traditional

00:44:14.510 --> 00:44:17.860 align:middle line:84%
game play point of view, that
sounds like a great idea.

00:44:17.860 --> 00:44:24.730 align:middle line:84%
That gets you back into the
game, whereas any very

00:44:24.730 --> 00:44:27.481 align:middle line:84%
realistic fight mechanic is
really probably going to be a

00:44:27.481 --> 00:44:28.690 align:middle line:90%
positive feedback mechanic.

00:44:28.690 --> 00:44:30.270 align:middle line:90%
You win, you keep winning.

00:44:30.270 --> 00:44:33.920 align:middle line:90%


00:44:33.920 --> 00:44:40.340 align:middle line:84%
You can think of these five
different things as--its not

00:44:40.340 --> 00:44:43.280 align:middle line:84%
like game balance, but these are
decisions that you have to

00:44:43.280 --> 00:44:46.785 align:middle line:84%
make either as an individual
designer or as a team about

00:44:46.785 --> 00:44:50.010 align:middle line:84%
what any given mechanic in
your game is trying to

00:44:50.010 --> 00:44:50.720 align:middle line:90%
accomplish.

00:44:50.720 --> 00:44:52.585 align:middle line:84%
It's rarely mutually
exclusive.

00:44:52.585 --> 00:44:55.310 align:middle line:84%
It's usually trying to involve
multiple one of these things.

00:44:55.310 --> 00:44:58.230 align:middle line:84%
You don't want to fall down on
any of these, especially not

00:44:58.230 --> 00:44:59.400 align:middle line:90%
feasibility.

00:44:59.400 --> 00:45:01.330 align:middle line:84%
It's like, yes this is the
perfect game mechanic!

00:45:01.330 --> 00:45:05.540 align:middle line:84%
And we had two years to be able
to put together all the

00:45:05.540 --> 00:45:07.930 align:middle line:90%
things that we had.

00:45:07.930 --> 00:45:09.790 align:middle line:84%
And for the games that
have failed to run

00:45:09.790 --> 00:45:12.280 align:middle line:90%
because of that idea.

00:45:12.280 --> 00:45:14.925 align:middle line:84%
Especially live-action games
that have a hidden computer

00:45:14.925 --> 00:45:19.100 align:middle line:84%
component, especially if
they're designed by MIT

00:45:19.100 --> 00:45:22.460 align:middle line:84%
students, rapidly designed into
a software engineering

00:45:22.460 --> 00:45:25.680 align:middle line:90%
project that never finishes.

00:45:25.680 --> 00:45:28.070 align:middle line:84%
The game kind of disappears and
it's all about this damn

00:45:28.070 --> 00:45:31.890 align:middle line:84%
website that would have solved
everything else.

00:45:31.890 --> 00:45:33.750 align:middle line:84%
It's completely realistic
because of the hacking

00:45:33.750 --> 00:45:36.100 align:middle line:84%
mechanic that actually happens
on the computer.

00:45:36.100 --> 00:45:39.800 align:middle line:84%
And the skills scale to your
ability as a character which

00:45:39.800 --> 00:45:41.900 align:middle line:84%
the computer already knows,
and you don't have to

00:45:41.900 --> 00:45:45.120 align:middle line:84%
remember, so you can
feel really neat.

00:45:45.120 --> 00:45:48.420 align:middle line:84%
And it's competitive because
you know what the opponents

00:45:48.420 --> 00:45:51.493 align:middle line:84%
are doing, but not everything
they're doing, and it's going

00:45:51.493 --> 00:45:53.734 align:middle line:84%
to take us five years
to write this code.

00:45:53.734 --> 00:45:55.198 align:middle line:84%
The game's never going
to happen.

00:45:55.198 --> 00:45:58.920 align:middle line:90%


00:45:58.920 --> 00:46:02.160 align:middle line:84%
Let's see, let me think about an
example just to sort of put

00:46:02.160 --> 00:46:05.820 align:middle line:90%
some ideas in your head.

00:46:05.820 --> 00:46:08.650 align:middle line:84%
Back to the idea of things
that players might be

00:46:08.650 --> 00:46:11.060 align:middle line:84%
uncomfortable doing
with each other.

00:46:11.060 --> 00:46:14.600 align:middle line:84%
Seduction is one of those
things that's--

00:46:14.600 --> 00:46:18.160 align:middle line:84%
if you don't have a game
mechanic for it, it never

00:46:18.160 --> 00:46:21.380 align:middle line:84%
happens in the way that
you want it to

00:46:21.380 --> 00:46:23.530 align:middle line:90%
that helps the game.

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.540 align:middle line:84%
If it happens, it's happening
between the players and that

00:46:26.540 --> 00:46:29.080 align:middle line:90%
causes all kinds of problems.

00:46:29.080 --> 00:46:32.180 align:middle line:84%
So you come up with a rule, come
up with a game mechanic,

00:46:32.180 --> 00:46:34.660 align:middle line:84%
that feels nothing like
real-life seduction.

00:46:34.660 --> 00:46:37.827 align:middle line:84%
We're going to play a game of
blackjack now, if I win you're

00:46:37.827 --> 00:46:38.935 align:middle line:90%
now in love with me.

00:46:38.935 --> 00:46:42.690 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, that's not how
seduction actually works, but

00:46:42.690 --> 00:46:44.910 align:middle line:84%
it actually makes it easier
for players to

00:46:44.910 --> 00:46:47.630 align:middle line:90%
say, I can do that.

00:46:47.630 --> 00:46:48.440 align:middle line:84%
I can play a game
of blackjack.

00:46:48.440 --> 00:46:49.690 align:middle line:84%
There's no social
awkwardness in

00:46:49.690 --> 00:46:50.400 align:middle line:90%
playing a game of blackjack.

00:46:50.400 --> 00:46:54.460 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You can associate that
with, I win this game so

00:46:54.460 --> 00:46:57.281 align:middle line:84%
I slipped inside your glass a
love potion so now you're in

00:46:57.281 --> 00:46:58.109 align:middle line:90%
love with me.

00:46:58.109 --> 00:46:59.359 align:middle line:90%
Something like that.

00:46:59.359 --> 00:47:02.036 align:middle line:90%


00:47:02.036 --> 00:47:04.260 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: But see, in that
particular case you're

00:47:04.260 --> 00:47:10.360 align:middle line:84%
describing an action that people
might be more actually

00:47:10.360 --> 00:47:13.820 align:middle line:84%
willing to do through some
sort of simple mechanic.

00:47:13.820 --> 00:47:15.800 align:middle line:84%
Like OK, instead of love potion
we're going to mechanic

00:47:15.800 --> 00:47:18.100 align:middle line:90%
that enables you to something.

00:47:18.100 --> 00:47:20.220 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to actually
give you a file.

00:47:20.220 --> 00:47:24.010 align:middle line:84%
And if you drink something in
the tea, like maple syrup,

00:47:24.010 --> 00:47:24.870 align:middle line:90%
talk to the GM.

00:47:24.870 --> 00:47:30.540 align:middle line:84%
Because you probably just
drank some magic potion.

00:47:30.540 --> 00:47:32.740 align:middle line:84%
But if the goal is, all
right we're playing

00:47:32.740 --> 00:47:33.870 align:middle line:90%
a film noire game.

00:47:33.870 --> 00:47:38.330 align:middle line:84%
And you're supposed to be
engaging in constant witty

00:47:38.330 --> 00:47:43.460 align:middle line:84%
banter, laden with sexual
innuendo, a lot of people have

00:47:43.460 --> 00:47:46.570 align:middle line:90%
a lot of difficulty with that.

00:47:46.570 --> 00:47:48.120 align:middle line:90%
Sometimes it works.

00:47:48.120 --> 00:47:50.970 align:middle line:90%
I made it work in some games.

00:47:50.970 --> 00:47:54.750 align:middle line:84%
But it makes it a lot easier if
the success or failure in

00:47:54.750 --> 00:48:00.020 align:middle line:84%
that is not going to be
bottlenecked by whether the

00:48:00.020 --> 00:48:03.210 align:middle line:84%
players feel comfortable
doing it.

00:48:03.210 --> 00:48:06.030 align:middle line:84%
So creating a game mechanic that
actually feels a little

00:48:06.030 --> 00:48:12.060 align:middle line:84%
bit less than what activity
you're trying to represent may

00:48:12.060 --> 00:48:14.790 align:middle line:84%
make it easier for people to
engage in the activity.

00:48:14.790 --> 00:48:16.680 align:middle line:84%
Same thing goes in combat,
playing rock, paper, scissors,

00:48:16.680 --> 00:48:18.890 align:middle line:84%
feels nothing like
an actual fight.

00:48:18.890 --> 00:48:21.110 align:middle line:84%
But people will be much more
willing to play rock, paper,

00:48:21.110 --> 00:48:23.170 align:middle line:84%
scissors with each other than
to punch each other.

00:48:23.170 --> 00:48:28.040 align:middle line:84%
So come up a rule, give them a
little plastic gun, give them

00:48:28.040 --> 00:48:30.914 align:middle line:84%
something to make it easier,
and they'll be willing to

00:48:30.914 --> 00:48:34.000 align:middle line:84%
engage in that activity
more often.

00:48:34.000 --> 00:48:36.817 align:middle line:84%
Violence in the rule will
probably be the thing that

00:48:36.817 --> 00:48:38.067 align:middle line:84%
comes up a lot in
this setting.

00:48:38.067 --> 00:48:44.730 align:middle line:90%


00:48:44.730 --> 00:48:51.208 align:middle line:84%
So, let me see what else
we can get into.

00:48:51.208 --> 00:48:54.620 align:middle line:90%
It's about 2 o'clock.

00:48:54.620 --> 00:48:59.050 align:middle line:84%
How many folks here have played
some variant of Mafia

00:48:59.050 --> 00:49:04.050 align:middle line:90%
or Werewolf or?

00:49:04.050 --> 00:49:06.770 align:middle line:84%
Who's played Mafia
in particular?

00:49:06.770 --> 00:49:08.470 align:middle line:90%
OK, who's played Werewolf?

00:49:08.470 --> 00:49:12.960 align:middle line:90%


00:49:12.960 --> 00:49:15.815 align:middle line:84%
So those are live-action
games too.

00:49:15.815 --> 00:49:18.630 align:middle line:84%
And those are live-action games
that specifically don't

00:49:18.630 --> 00:49:23.860 align:middle line:84%
try to draw too sharp a division
between who you are

00:49:23.860 --> 00:49:25.350 align:middle line:90%
and who your player is.

00:49:25.350 --> 00:49:30.625 align:middle line:84%
You are given a role, a card,
that basically says whether

00:49:30.625 --> 00:49:34.960 align:middle line:84%
you're Mafia or whether you're
a villager I think.

00:49:34.960 --> 00:49:36.290 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Civilian.

00:49:36.290 --> 00:49:40.250 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: And the whole idea
is that everybody who's--

00:49:40.250 --> 00:49:43.690 align:middle line:84%
that's a character sheet that
gives you private, secret,

00:49:43.690 --> 00:49:46.840 align:middle line:84%
information about the role that
you're supposed to play.

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:53.010 align:middle line:84%
But in my opinion, these
particular games-- and we're

00:49:53.010 --> 00:49:54.940 align:middle line:84%
going to play some variants
of them--

00:49:54.940 --> 00:49:58.400 align:middle line:90%


00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:02.020 align:middle line:84%
what you are told by the
cards is only what

00:50:02.020 --> 00:50:03.720 align:middle line:90%
you're allowed to do.

00:50:03.720 --> 00:50:08.520 align:middle line:84%
What actually gives you the role
is what you decide to do.

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:13.140 align:middle line:84%
I play Mafia and I'm going to
say, Patrick needs to die.

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:19.179 align:middle line:84%
And other people in the room
agree with me, and Patrick's

00:50:19.179 --> 00:50:20.610 align:middle line:84%
character gets taken
out of the game.

00:50:20.610 --> 00:50:23.800 align:middle line:84%
That decision is the decision
that made me Mafia.

00:50:23.800 --> 00:50:26.540 align:middle line:84%
And that decision is the
decision that other people are

00:50:26.540 --> 00:50:33.650 align:middle line:84%
trying to catch, because they
are trying to find a person

00:50:33.650 --> 00:50:36.090 align:middle line:84%
who's actually responsible for
various acts when everybody's

00:50:36.090 --> 00:50:37.560 align:middle line:90%
eyes are closed.

00:50:37.560 --> 00:50:42.290 align:middle line:84%
The general model for these
games is there are cycles.

00:50:42.290 --> 00:50:45.120 align:middle line:84%
For Werewolf it's a day and
night cycle, and for Mafia

00:50:45.120 --> 00:50:46.560 align:middle line:90%
it's also day and night.

00:50:46.560 --> 00:50:51.090 align:middle line:84%
But the idea is that during the
night everybody who's not

00:50:51.090 --> 00:50:54.270 align:middle line:84%
Mafia gets to have
their eyes open.

00:50:54.270 --> 00:50:56.180 align:middle line:90%
Everybody who is Mafia--

00:50:56.180 --> 00:50:58.280 align:middle line:84%
everybody who's not Mafia
keeps their eyes closed.

00:50:58.280 --> 00:51:01.080 align:middle line:84%
Everyone who is Mafia keeps
their eyes open.

00:51:01.080 --> 00:51:05.550 align:middle line:84%
And people who are Mafia can
communicate through some GM

00:51:05.550 --> 00:51:09.090 align:middle line:84%
mediator, some game master
mediated way, to specify the

00:51:09.090 --> 00:51:11.650 align:middle line:84%
player to be taken out of
the game at this point.

00:51:11.650 --> 00:51:12.950 align:middle line:90%
It's usually a point.

00:51:12.950 --> 00:51:15.710 align:middle line:90%


00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:18.400 align:middle line:90%
I see things with blinks.

00:51:18.400 --> 00:51:20.770 align:middle line:84%
Because as the GM moves around
sometimes that gives you

00:51:20.770 --> 00:51:22.460 align:middle line:90%
clues, because you can hear.

00:51:22.460 --> 00:51:25.110 align:middle line:84%
Even with your eyes closed you
can hear where the GM is.

00:51:25.110 --> 00:51:29.410 align:middle line:90%


00:51:29.410 --> 00:51:32.180 align:middle line:84%
And then that's like
the night cycle.

00:51:32.180 --> 00:51:36.985 align:middle line:84%
And during the day cycle
everyone opens their eyes and

00:51:36.985 --> 00:51:39.940 align:middle line:84%
whoever's been taken out of
the game informs you.

00:51:39.940 --> 00:51:43.670 align:middle line:84%
And everyone knows and everybody
starts discussing

00:51:43.670 --> 00:51:46.160 align:middle line:84%
and accusing each other of
being responsible for it.

00:51:46.160 --> 00:51:49.132 align:middle line:84%
And the whole point of the game
is for the villagers to

00:51:49.132 --> 00:51:51.380 align:middle line:84%
identify who all the Mafia is
before everyone finishes.

00:51:51.380 --> 00:51:58.380 align:middle line:90%


00:51:58.380 --> 00:52:01.210 align:middle line:84%
It's a live-action game that
doesn't have a lot of action.

00:52:01.210 --> 00:52:03.570 align:middle line:90%
I would say, it's one action.

00:52:03.570 --> 00:52:04.910 align:middle line:84%
There's closing your
eyes, opening your

00:52:04.910 --> 00:52:07.990 align:middle line:90%
eyes, and there's pointing.

00:52:07.990 --> 00:52:10.330 align:middle line:90%
There are variants where

00:52:10.330 --> 00:52:13.590 align:middle line:90%
sometimes there is a Godfather.

00:52:13.590 --> 00:52:16.430 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I've seen websites
with whole lists of different

00:52:16.430 --> 00:52:18.000 align:middle line:84%
roles that are just
ridiculous.

00:52:18.000 --> 00:52:21.640 align:middle line:84%
Like there's grave keeper,
there's necromancers.

00:52:21.640 --> 00:52:23.170 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: There's people
who can bring characters

00:52:23.170 --> 00:52:24.480 align:middle line:90%
back from the dead.

00:52:24.480 --> 00:52:26.730 align:middle line:84%
There are people who
can basically do

00:52:26.730 --> 00:52:28.170 align:middle line:90%
a background check.

00:52:28.170 --> 00:52:28.910 align:middle line:90%
I think that's--

00:52:28.910 --> 00:52:30.260 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, that's
right you told us.

00:52:30.260 --> 00:52:31.510 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: -- angels and cops.

00:52:31.510 --> 00:52:34.810 align:middle line:90%


00:52:34.810 --> 00:52:38.470 align:middle line:84%
And you go from two cycles to
three cycles per round.

00:52:38.470 --> 00:52:39.802 align:middle line:84%
Four cycles per round where
different people

00:52:39.802 --> 00:52:41.290 align:middle line:90%
have their eyes open.

00:52:41.290 --> 00:52:46.620 align:middle line:84%
And you get different bits of
information about what the

00:52:46.620 --> 00:52:49.090 align:middle line:90%
world state is like.

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:53.830 align:middle line:84%
The fact that killing somebody
is pointing at them, that's a

00:52:53.830 --> 00:52:55.260 align:middle line:90%
dissociation mechanic right?

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:57.365 align:middle line:84%
It's not like you're actually
being asked to engage in

00:52:57.365 --> 00:52:59.378 align:middle line:90%
physical combat.

00:52:59.378 --> 00:53:01.745 align:middle line:84%
Even though the fiction of
the game is that you're

00:53:01.745 --> 00:53:03.200 align:middle line:84%
assassinating this person in
the middle of the night.

00:53:03.200 --> 00:53:06.640 align:middle line:90%


00:53:06.640 --> 00:53:10.435 align:middle line:84%
The verisimilitude, and
competition, will largely come

00:53:10.435 --> 00:53:12.870 align:middle line:90%
down to the discussion phase.

00:53:12.870 --> 00:53:17.970 align:middle line:84%
They are trying to put players
in an environment where you've

00:53:17.970 --> 00:53:22.660 align:middle line:84%
got a paranoid of villagers, a
paranoid town, and they're all

00:53:22.660 --> 00:53:27.390 align:middle line:90%
mutually accusing each other.

00:53:27.390 --> 00:53:34.240 align:middle line:84%
And both the act of trying to
not be accused, and trying to

00:53:34.240 --> 00:53:40.210 align:middle line:84%
deduce who is the right person
to accuse, is a compelling

00:53:40.210 --> 00:53:41.730 align:middle line:90%
competitive experience.

00:53:41.730 --> 00:53:45.360 align:middle line:84%
That's what all these games
are really about.

00:53:45.360 --> 00:53:48.820 align:middle line:84%
So all the emphasis, all the
time of the game frankly, is

00:53:48.820 --> 00:53:52.520 align:middle line:84%
devoted to that phase when
everyone sits down and

00:53:52.520 --> 00:53:56.820 align:middle line:84%
discusses with each other
who could be the Mafia.

00:53:56.820 --> 00:54:01.126 align:middle line:84%
You want to walk us through
one of the variants maybe?

00:54:01.126 --> 00:54:03.260 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah, do you
want to actually play it?

00:54:03.260 --> 00:54:05.640 align:middle line:90%
Or just describe it?

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:09.540 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: We have 1, 2,
3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9,

00:54:09.540 --> 00:54:11.890 align:middle line:90%
10, 11, 12, 13, 14.

00:54:11.890 --> 00:54:14.050 align:middle line:84%
We could have two groups
or one big group.

00:54:14.050 --> 00:54:15.690 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: One big group
may take awhile.

00:54:15.690 --> 00:54:18.424 align:middle line:90%


00:54:18.424 --> 00:54:22.220 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Let's do two
groups but how many

00:54:22.220 --> 00:54:23.840 align:middle line:90%
different rule variants?

00:54:23.840 --> 00:54:25.740 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I only found one
that I thought was really

00:54:25.740 --> 00:54:28.192 align:middle line:84%
interesting, although we can
certainly-- even the Wikipedia

00:54:28.192 --> 00:54:30.210 align:middle line:90%
page has a million variants.

00:54:30.210 --> 00:54:32.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The one I normally
would play would be one where

00:54:32.620 --> 00:54:34.950 align:middle line:84%
there's mafia and
there's cops.

00:54:34.950 --> 00:54:36.490 align:middle line:90%
There might be a medic.

00:54:36.490 --> 00:54:40.718 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Inspector, angel,
and prostitute.

00:54:40.718 --> 00:54:42.160 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: What's the prostitute
do again?

00:54:42.160 --> 00:54:42.656 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you sleep
with the mafia

00:54:42.656 --> 00:54:43.420 align:middle line:90%
they don't kill anybody.

00:54:43.420 --> 00:54:46.070 align:middle line:84%
If you sleep with an inspector
they get incorrect

00:54:46.070 --> 00:54:46.560 align:middle line:90%
information.

00:54:46.560 --> 00:54:48.770 align:middle line:84%
If you sleep with a nurse
they don't heal.

00:54:48.770 --> 00:54:54.570 align:middle line:90%


00:54:54.570 --> 00:54:56.370 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: This is it's very
important to make sure you

00:54:56.370 --> 00:54:57.980 align:middle line:84%
know what rules that
you're playing with

00:54:57.980 --> 00:55:00.500 align:middle line:90%
before the game starts.

00:55:00.500 --> 00:55:04.220 align:middle line:84%
So walk us through the roles,
and then what we'll do is

00:55:04.220 --> 00:55:06.450 align:middle line:84%
we'll break this into
two groups.

00:55:06.450 --> 00:55:13.030 align:middle line:84%
And we'll have two groups play
separately and that's probably

00:55:13.030 --> 00:55:16.800 align:middle line:90%
the end of the class activity.

00:55:16.800 --> 00:55:20.760 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: I had found a
variant called Thing, which is

00:55:20.760 --> 00:55:23.200 align:middle line:84%
based on the John
Carpenter movie.

00:55:23.200 --> 00:55:25.440 align:middle line:84%
Which is interesting because it
takes out one of the roles.

00:55:25.440 --> 00:55:30.260 align:middle line:84%
The idea is one person is the
shape shifting thing, and at

00:55:30.260 --> 00:55:33.300 align:middle line:84%
night they infect someone else
and that person becomes a

00:55:33.300 --> 00:55:34.980 align:middle line:90%
thing as well.

00:55:34.980 --> 00:55:37.860 align:middle line:84%
So they're not knocked
out, out right.

00:55:37.860 --> 00:55:40.930 align:middle line:84%
And then during the day the
group votes and decides on one

00:55:40.930 --> 00:55:42.580 align:middle line:90%
person to test.

00:55:42.580 --> 00:55:45.720 align:middle line:84%
And if they test a thing that
person is killed and they get

00:55:45.720 --> 00:55:47.020 align:middle line:90%
to test another one.

00:55:47.020 --> 00:55:49.410 align:middle line:84%
And the idea is that the things
are trying to outnumber

00:55:49.410 --> 00:55:51.810 align:middle line:90%
the people.

00:55:51.810 --> 00:55:53.540 align:middle line:84%
Which is interesting because
it takes away

00:55:53.540 --> 00:55:56.840 align:middle line:90%
that inspector role.

00:55:56.840 --> 00:56:00.552 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you know who
gets thinged in the

00:56:00.552 --> 00:56:02.120 align:middle line:90%
middle of the night?

00:56:02.120 --> 00:56:03.520 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: So, I was
thinking about this.

00:56:03.520 --> 00:56:07.140 align:middle line:84%
What you would need is for the
person who is the thing to

00:56:07.140 --> 00:56:08.810 align:middle line:90%
indicate to the moderator.

00:56:08.810 --> 00:56:12.460 align:middle line:84%
And then maybe the moderator
could tap them or something.

00:56:12.460 --> 00:56:14.526 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Ok, so if you're a
thing you know you're a thing.

00:56:14.526 --> 00:56:16.770 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yeah.

00:56:16.770 --> 00:56:19.020 align:middle line:84%
The things are spreading
as opposed to just--

00:56:19.020 --> 00:56:21.060 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And then if there's
two things, do they both just

00:56:21.060 --> 00:56:22.290 align:middle line:90%
agree on another?

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:22.530 align:middle line:90%
On a third?

00:56:22.530 --> 00:56:23.460 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Presumably.

00:56:23.460 --> 00:56:27.360 align:middle line:84%
So this is really interesting
because apparently this is

00:56:27.360 --> 00:56:30.210 align:middle line:84%
played at a conference for
science fiction writers.

00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:34.155 align:middle line:84%
So there's little snippets of it
on the web about the rules

00:56:34.155 --> 00:56:35.590 align:middle line:84%
from different blogs
and stuff.

00:56:35.590 --> 00:56:37.510 align:middle line:84%
And it's not as interesting, but
of course the problem is

00:56:37.510 --> 00:56:39.810 align:middle line:84%
how do you Google
for mafia thing?

00:56:39.810 --> 00:56:44.590 align:middle line:90%


00:56:44.590 --> 00:56:47.370 align:middle line:84%
It's not interesting to me, and
then it seems like there's

00:56:47.370 --> 00:56:48.620 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

00:56:48.620 --> 00:56:52.020 align:middle line:90%


00:56:52.020 --> 00:56:55.100 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So it might be fine
actually to have two groups

00:56:55.100 --> 00:56:58.296 align:middle line:84%
figure out how to play that game
just from the description

00:56:58.296 --> 00:56:59.940 align:middle line:90%
that you've just been given.

00:56:59.940 --> 00:57:01.490 align:middle line:90%
I think [INAUDIBLE]

00:57:01.490 --> 00:57:02.872 align:middle line:84%
there's going to be enough
people who've played

00:57:02.872 --> 00:57:05.100 align:middle line:84%
traditional Mafia to
know how it works.

00:57:05.100 --> 00:57:09.580 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll just reconvene at
the end of it and see what

00:57:09.580 --> 00:57:12.330 align:middle line:84%
each version people
came up with.

00:57:12.330 --> 00:57:15.670 align:middle line:84%
What were the similarities and
what were the differences.

00:57:15.670 --> 00:57:18.160 align:middle line:84%
Again, that's the flexibility
of doing a live-action game

00:57:18.160 --> 00:57:19.670 align:middle line:84%
when the people who are actually
coming up the the

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:23.070 align:middle line:84%
rules are actually in the same
room as the players, or are

00:57:23.070 --> 00:57:24.595 align:middle line:90%
the players themselves.

00:57:24.595 --> 00:57:26.250 align:middle line:84%
You get to make stuff
up on the fly as

00:57:26.250 --> 00:57:27.500 align:middle line:90%
long as everyone agrees.

00:57:27.500 --> 00:57:29.870 align:middle line:90%


00:57:29.870 --> 00:57:31.530 align:middle line:90%
So let's give it a shot.

00:57:31.530 --> 00:57:36.516 align:middle line:84%
While Jason is sorting out, any
questions about the stuff?

00:57:36.516 --> 00:57:42.578 align:middle line:90%


00:57:42.578 --> 00:57:45.554 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: The two biggest
challenges that I've come

00:57:45.554 --> 00:57:52.810 align:middle line:84%
across in the block mechanics
have to do with surgical

00:57:52.810 --> 00:57:57.610 align:middle line:84%
challenges and intellectual
skill.

00:57:57.610 --> 00:58:00.076 align:middle line:90%
So let's go intellectual.

00:58:00.076 --> 00:58:02.580 align:middle line:84%
It's easy to play a character
that's stupider than you.

00:58:02.580 --> 00:58:04.710 align:middle line:84%
It's really hard to play
a character that's

00:58:04.710 --> 00:58:05.820 align:middle line:90%
smarter than you.

00:58:05.820 --> 00:58:08.390 align:middle line:84%
In particular, it's really
hard to play a character

00:58:08.390 --> 00:58:11.980 align:middle line:84%
that's smarter than you if you
don't have a GM around all the

00:58:11.980 --> 00:58:15.770 align:middle line:90%
time to tell you, stop.

00:58:15.770 --> 00:58:18.686 align:middle line:90%


00:58:18.686 --> 00:58:21.225 align:middle line:84%
So, if you have any ideas about
how to deal with that.

00:58:21.225 --> 00:58:26.306 align:middle line:84%
The other one is, I'm creating
social conflicts.

00:58:26.306 --> 00:58:29.810 align:middle line:84%
Like, this is harder than any
role-playing game because it

00:58:29.810 --> 00:58:33.120 align:middle line:84%
feels like if you want to be
able to persuade a character

00:58:33.120 --> 00:58:35.768 align:middle line:84%
or something you just want to
persuade them because you're

00:58:35.768 --> 00:58:37.640 align:middle line:90%
role-playing right?

00:58:37.640 --> 00:58:39.960 align:middle line:84%
But you're supposed to be
playing a character who is

00:58:39.960 --> 00:58:41.990 align:middle line:90%
more persuasive than you.

00:58:41.990 --> 00:58:44.870 align:middle line:90%
But it feels really cheap.

00:58:44.870 --> 00:58:46.620 align:middle line:90%
If you can just sort of.

00:58:46.620 --> 00:58:49.480 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: You believe me now!

00:58:49.480 --> 00:58:53.190 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It also ties into
another area that frequently

00:58:53.190 --> 00:58:55.635 align:middle line:84%
pops up in LARP's and
role-playing games in general

00:58:55.635 --> 00:58:58.210 align:middle line:84%
which is use of metagame
knowledge.

00:58:58.210 --> 00:59:03.030 align:middle line:84%
So frequently if you've been
persuaded to do something then

00:59:03.030 --> 00:59:07.400 align:middle line:84%
you've come across players using
their metagame knowledge

00:59:07.400 --> 00:59:09.420 align:middle line:84%
that they don't want to do the
thing that they're being

00:59:09.420 --> 00:59:11.720 align:middle line:90%
mechanically forced to do.

00:59:11.720 --> 00:59:15.100 align:middle line:84%
And they're trying to find
loopholes constantly.

00:59:15.100 --> 00:59:20.490 align:middle line:84%
I don't know if you can really
force people to abandon their

00:59:20.490 --> 00:59:21.960 align:middle line:84%
metagame knowledge
mechanically?

00:59:21.960 --> 00:59:24.410 align:middle line:90%
But I don't know.

00:59:24.410 --> 00:59:28.090 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I have some examples
on how the Assassin's

00:59:28.090 --> 00:59:32.690 align:middle line:84%
Guild in particular has
dealt with that.

00:59:32.690 --> 00:59:36.580 align:middle line:84%
And in some ways it's cheating,
because we're not

00:59:36.580 --> 00:59:39.370 align:middle line:84%
going to try to do some
of those things.

00:59:39.370 --> 00:59:41.650 align:middle line:84%
In particular, let's
take persuasion for

00:59:41.650 --> 00:59:43.450 align:middle line:90%
one of those things.

00:59:43.450 --> 00:59:47.020 align:middle line:84%
The problem with persuasion is
that whatever mechanic you

00:59:47.020 --> 00:59:51.110 align:middle line:84%
come to, other than what you
see is what you get--

00:59:51.110 --> 00:59:52.360 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE]--

00:59:52.360 --> 00:59:56.750 align:middle line:90%


00:59:56.750 --> 00:59:59.900 align:middle line:84%
any other mechanic that you come
up with runs that risk of

00:59:59.900 --> 01:00:01.640 align:middle line:84%
the, wait a minute this
is not what I really,

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:03.460 align:middle line:90%
really, want to do.

01:00:03.460 --> 01:00:07.190 align:middle line:84%
So the Assassin's Guild
has replaced that with

01:00:07.190 --> 01:00:07.810 align:middle line:90%
brainwashing.

01:00:07.810 --> 01:00:12.790 align:middle line:84%
Which is the whole idea of,
you're not being persuaded.

01:00:12.790 --> 01:00:16.870 align:middle line:84%
You goals are being forcibly
changed, but now you have

01:00:16.870 --> 01:00:18.470 align:middle line:90%
other goals.

01:00:18.470 --> 01:00:19.720 align:middle line:90%
And that's the big difference.

01:00:19.720 --> 01:00:23.390 align:middle line:90%


01:00:23.390 --> 01:00:26.300 align:middle line:84%
Your old goals are no longer
your win conditions.

01:00:26.300 --> 01:00:29.160 align:middle line:84%
Your new goals are now
your win conditions.

01:00:29.160 --> 01:00:33.247 align:middle line:84%
You can still win the game by
obeying your new goals.

01:00:33.247 --> 01:00:35.430 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: That's cool.

01:00:35.430 --> 01:00:37.144 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: The brainwashing
mechanism.

01:00:37.144 --> 01:00:39.154 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So you've aligned the
interests of the player

01:00:39.154 --> 01:00:41.410 align:middle line:84%
with the interest of
the character.

01:00:41.410 --> 01:00:46.680 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR:You want to go with
your new goals now because--

01:00:46.680 --> 01:00:50.110 align:middle line:84%
how this came up, typically in
the Assassin's Guild, the

01:00:50.110 --> 01:00:51.940 align:middle line:84%
Assassin's Guild actually
grew out from

01:00:51.940 --> 01:00:53.250 align:middle line:90%
circle of death games.

01:00:53.250 --> 01:00:54.950 align:middle line:90%
Which is, getting to know you.

01:00:54.950 --> 01:00:57.175 align:middle line:84%
I have the name of somebody, and
I need to shoot them with

01:00:57.175 --> 01:00:58.430 align:middle line:90%
this water gun.

01:00:58.430 --> 01:00:59.950 align:middle line:90%
Who is this person again?

01:00:59.950 --> 01:01:00.450 align:middle line:90%
I don't know.

01:01:00.450 --> 01:01:01.230 align:middle line:90%
I'm a freshman.

01:01:01.230 --> 01:01:02.010 align:middle line:84%
I haven't met everyone
on campus.

01:01:02.010 --> 01:01:03.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to figure out
who this person is.

01:01:03.180 --> 01:01:05.560 align:middle line:84%
That's where those
games came from.

01:01:05.560 --> 01:01:07.440 align:middle line:84%
The Assassin's Guild kind
of grew up from that.

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:09.200 align:middle line:90%
Then it became teams.

01:01:09.200 --> 01:01:14.190 align:middle line:84%
And brainwashing was basically
the set of game mechanics that

01:01:14.190 --> 01:01:16.140 align:middle line:84%
was used for recruiting
people to your team.

01:01:16.140 --> 01:01:19.010 align:middle line:84%
So it's like, all right
everyone's got one target.

01:01:19.010 --> 01:01:22.490 align:middle line:84%
But there are a few people out
there who's job is destroying

01:01:22.490 --> 01:01:25.730 align:middle line:84%
everybody on this opposing
faction.

01:01:25.730 --> 01:01:27.820 align:middle line:84%
And there's only one person
in that opposing

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:28.430 align:middle line:90%
faction right now.

01:01:28.430 --> 01:01:29.060 align:middle line:90%
There's only one of me.

01:01:29.060 --> 01:01:31.750 align:middle line:84%
But I have this brainwashing
mechanic that recruits more

01:01:31.750 --> 01:01:32.320 align:middle line:90%
and more people.

01:01:32.320 --> 01:01:35.810 align:middle line:84%
So what you thought was one,
I'm just against another

01:01:35.810 --> 01:01:40.500 align:middle line:84%
individual, becomes my group
is against that group.

01:01:40.500 --> 01:01:42.160 align:middle line:84%
And it feels pretty
awesome, actually.

01:01:42.160 --> 01:01:43.820 align:middle line:84%
Because what it actually
feels--

01:01:43.820 --> 01:01:48.620 align:middle line:84%
it's a weird game mechanic way
of making your character have

01:01:48.620 --> 01:01:50.960 align:middle line:84%
some sort of development
in the game.

01:01:50.960 --> 01:01:53.960 align:middle line:84%
If you think about a
role-playing game, character

01:01:53.960 --> 01:01:57.210 align:middle line:84%
development means your goals
now change somehow.

01:01:57.210 --> 01:02:01.340 align:middle line:84%
But it's really difficult to
give players a means of doing

01:02:01.340 --> 01:02:02.840 align:middle line:90%
that themselves.

01:02:02.840 --> 01:02:04.270 align:middle line:84%
Brainwashing was one of the
few ways that seems to

01:02:04.270 --> 01:02:05.570 align:middle line:90%
actually have worked.

01:02:05.570 --> 01:02:08.780 align:middle line:84%
I'm actively going to knock out
this character, put this

01:02:08.780 --> 01:02:12.130 align:middle line:84%
blinky helmet on them for five
seconds, and if nobody catches

01:02:12.130 --> 01:02:14.570 align:middle line:84%
me in this process I give
them new goals.

01:02:14.570 --> 01:02:17.260 align:middle line:84%
And the person who's
[INAUDIBLE], dude this is way

01:02:17.260 --> 01:02:19.074 align:middle line:84%
more exciting than anything
[INAUDIBLE].

01:02:19.074 --> 01:02:21.790 align:middle line:90%


01:02:21.790 --> 01:02:25.770 align:middle line:84%
And you also avoid the metagame
problem of loyalty.

01:02:25.770 --> 01:02:28.370 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I got this
information from this other

01:02:28.370 --> 01:02:30.720 align:middle line:84%
group but I've been brainwashed
and I don't want

01:02:30.720 --> 01:02:32.090 align:middle line:90%
to tell you.

01:02:32.090 --> 01:02:33.250 align:middle line:84%
It's like, no you want to tell
me now because it'll actually

01:02:33.250 --> 01:02:34.500 align:middle line:90%
help you win.

01:02:34.500 --> 01:02:36.260 align:middle line:90%


01:02:36.260 --> 01:02:42.320 align:middle line:84%
But metagaming in all
kinds of live-action

01:02:42.320 --> 01:02:43.740 align:middle line:90%
games is a big thing.

01:02:43.740 --> 01:02:47.850 align:middle line:84%
You know things about the player
or you know things

01:02:47.850 --> 01:02:49.506 align:middle line:84%
about the game state
that you're not

01:02:49.506 --> 01:02:51.260 align:middle line:90%
really supposed to know.

01:02:51.260 --> 01:02:54.096 align:middle line:84%
If a person is supposed to be
able to teleport from one end

01:02:54.096 --> 01:02:56.610 align:middle line:84%
of campus to the other end of
campus, the character may be

01:02:56.610 --> 01:02:57.810 align:middle line:90%
able to do that.

01:02:57.810 --> 01:03:00.286 align:middle line:84%
But the damn player's got to
walk all the way in between.

01:03:00.286 --> 01:03:03.100 align:middle line:84%
And you have to drive the player
halfway to the, I now

01:03:03.100 --> 01:03:05.980 align:middle line:84%
know that player is probably
on this side of campus.

01:03:05.980 --> 01:03:08.298 align:middle line:84%
Even though your character
really had no idea how

01:03:08.298 --> 01:03:09.480 align:middle line:90%
to deal with that.

01:03:09.480 --> 01:03:12.345 align:middle line:90%
That's just one of those--

01:03:12.345 --> 01:03:16.880 align:middle line:84%
there are ways to design game
mechanics to avoid those

01:03:16.880 --> 01:03:17.560 align:middle line:90%
situations.

01:03:17.560 --> 01:03:21.630 align:middle line:84%
But in many, many, live-action
role playing there is a honor

01:03:21.630 --> 01:03:24.710 align:middle line:84%
system that really
has to kick in.

01:03:24.710 --> 01:03:26.580 align:middle line:84%
The nice thing about it is that
if everyone decides to go

01:03:26.580 --> 01:03:29.280 align:middle line:84%
by the honor system, and agrees
that this honor system

01:03:29.280 --> 01:03:32.750 align:middle line:84%
is necessary for the world to
be reinforced, it actually

01:03:32.750 --> 01:03:35.586 align:middle line:84%
helps reinforce that mutual
illusion that

01:03:35.586 --> 01:03:38.500 align:middle line:90%
this world is real.

01:03:38.500 --> 01:03:40.940 align:middle line:84%
By saying, I know that I'm not
supposed to know those things.

01:03:40.940 --> 01:03:48.330 align:middle line:84%
But I am going to play this game
as hard as I can in a way

01:03:48.330 --> 01:03:50.000 align:middle line:90%
that's [INAUDIBLE].

01:03:50.000 --> 01:03:52.590 align:middle line:84%
Actually reinforces the world
for everybody else.

01:03:52.590 --> 01:03:53.915 align:middle line:84%
It's the same for
improv theatre.

01:03:53.915 --> 01:03:56.454 align:middle line:90%


01:03:56.454 --> 01:04:00.850 align:middle line:84%
Anyone here involved in improv
theatre or improv comedy?

01:04:00.850 --> 01:04:03.100 align:middle line:90%
So, you know yes, and?

01:04:03.100 --> 01:04:04.130 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Sure.

01:04:04.130 --> 01:04:07.370 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: What would be the
concept behind that?

01:04:07.370 --> 01:04:10.010 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yes, and, is an idea
where if you give someone an

01:04:10.010 --> 01:04:13.580 align:middle line:90%
offer they'll take your offer.

01:04:13.580 --> 01:04:18.220 align:middle line:84%
So if I say, man we're
all out of tomatoes.

01:04:18.220 --> 01:04:22.700 align:middle line:84%
Someone will say, yes and the
big tomato cook-off is

01:04:22.700 --> 01:04:24.880 align:middle line:84%
tomorrow or something
like this.

01:04:24.880 --> 01:04:26.350 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You cannot contradict
what was said.

01:04:26.350 --> 01:04:28.410 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Not only do you not
contradict, you always add

01:04:28.410 --> 01:04:28.930 align:middle line:90%
information.

01:04:28.930 --> 01:04:30.943 align:middle line:84%
Every line, at least at the
beginning of the scene, should

01:04:30.943 --> 01:04:32.552 align:middle line:84%
be adding information
to the scene.

01:04:32.552 --> 01:04:35.300 align:middle line:84%
And then after a few lines you
have a very, very, elaborate

01:04:35.300 --> 01:04:37.950 align:middle line:84%
set up that's kind
of ridiculous.

01:04:37.950 --> 01:04:40.070 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: But, mutually
shared?

01:04:40.070 --> 01:04:41.760 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Exactly.

01:04:41.760 --> 01:04:44.220 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So, in that
same situation.

01:04:44.220 --> 01:04:47.570 align:middle line:84%
Metagaming is one of those
things that's actively working

01:04:47.570 --> 01:04:49.340 align:middle line:84%
to destroy the reality
of your world.

01:04:49.340 --> 01:04:54.740 align:middle line:84%
And if all the players agree to
try their damn hardest to

01:04:54.740 --> 01:04:57.160 align:middle line:84%
not let it interfere with
the world [INAUDIBLE].

01:04:57.160 --> 01:05:02.040 align:middle line:84%
The illusion in a way that it
strengthens the scene for

01:05:02.040 --> 01:05:04.838 align:middle line:90%
improv theatre.

01:05:04.838 --> 01:05:07.900 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Hold on, just one
sec, the other concern that

01:05:07.900 --> 01:05:11.630 align:middle line:84%
you had before persuasion
issue which was?

01:05:11.630 --> 01:05:17.042 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Characters who, in
particular this comes up a lot

01:05:17.042 --> 01:05:19.994 align:middle line:84%
in this [INAUDIBLE] type
situations or what have you,

01:05:19.994 --> 01:05:22.990 align:middle line:84%
where you have somebody's who
either a super genius.

01:05:22.990 --> 01:05:25.140 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Oh, so someone who's
better than you at something.

01:05:25.140 --> 01:05:28.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Much better
than you.

01:05:28.020 --> 01:05:30.900 align:middle line:90%
How do you mechanic that?

01:05:30.900 --> 01:05:33.920 align:middle line:90%


01:05:33.920 --> 01:05:36.600 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: The best way that you
can do that is to cast the

01:05:36.600 --> 01:05:37.530 align:middle line:90%
right person.

01:05:37.530 --> 01:05:44.000 align:middle line:84%
But what I have generally found
is that old role-playing

01:05:44.000 --> 01:05:47.510 align:middle line:84%
game chestnut of separating
intelligence from wisdom makes

01:05:47.510 --> 01:05:48.970 align:middle line:90%
it a little bit easier.

01:05:48.970 --> 01:05:51.034 align:middle line:84%
There's intelligence, which is
the ability to think on the

01:05:51.034 --> 01:05:52.550 align:middle line:84%
feet, and there's wisdom
which is how much you

01:05:52.550 --> 01:05:54.790 align:middle line:90%
know about the world.

01:05:54.790 --> 01:06:00.990 align:middle line:84%
And you as the game master
designing a game can figure

01:06:00.990 --> 01:06:03.995 align:middle line:84%
out, when I say someone's really
smart, what do I mean?

01:06:03.995 --> 01:06:05.090 align:middle line:90%
Intelligence or wisdom?

01:06:05.090 --> 01:06:08.410 align:middle line:84%
It helps you a little bit on
thinking how I can use game

01:06:08.410 --> 01:06:11.940 align:middle line:84%
mechanics or game writing
to fix that problem.

01:06:11.940 --> 01:06:15.360 align:middle line:84%
So if it's intelligence it's
just like, oh I have the

01:06:15.360 --> 01:06:18.190 align:middle line:84%
ability to do stuff that
other people can't do

01:06:18.190 --> 01:06:20.010 align:middle line:90%
because I'm so smart.

01:06:20.010 --> 01:06:24.740 align:middle line:84%
And you can literally just give
them abilities to act on

01:06:24.740 --> 01:06:28.440 align:middle line:84%
things that other people
do not have.

01:06:28.440 --> 01:06:31.730 align:middle line:84%
I'm able to build a telephone
out of this Coke because I'm a

01:06:31.730 --> 01:06:34.900 align:middle line:84%
professor of Gilligan's
Island.

01:06:34.900 --> 01:06:36.330 align:middle line:90%
I'm smart.

01:06:36.330 --> 01:06:37.920 align:middle line:84%
It doesn't mean I have to
act terribly smart.

01:06:37.920 --> 01:06:40.083 align:middle line:84%
I'm capable of doing things
that other people can't

01:06:40.083 --> 01:06:42.060 align:middle line:84%
because I've been told
specifically

01:06:42.060 --> 01:06:43.330 align:middle line:90%
I have these abilities.

01:06:43.330 --> 01:06:44.860 align:middle line:84%
But that's the intelligence
side of it.

01:06:44.860 --> 01:06:46.120 align:middle line:84%
The wisdom side of it is
a little bit harder.

01:06:46.120 --> 01:06:48.410 align:middle line:84%
Which is basically, you
dump a whole bunch of

01:06:48.410 --> 01:06:50.100 align:middle line:90%
information on somebody.

01:06:50.100 --> 01:06:53.600 align:middle line:84%
And you hope that they are
capable of making associations

01:06:53.600 --> 01:06:56.580 align:middle line:84%
between what's happening in
game and what they read.

01:06:56.580 --> 01:06:57.830 align:middle line:90%
That's a lot harder.

01:06:57.830 --> 01:07:02.890 align:middle line:84%
It is a problem, but I find that
by separating those two

01:07:02.890 --> 01:07:06.635 align:middle line:90%
it makes it an easier problem.

01:07:06.635 --> 01:07:08.132 align:middle line:90%
Patrick?

01:07:08.132 --> 01:07:10.770 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Yeah, this is a bit
of a noobish question.

01:07:10.770 --> 01:07:12.640 align:middle line:84%
Are LARPs usually played
in real time?

01:07:12.640 --> 01:07:15.860 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Typically, they have
rules about when to hit

01:07:15.860 --> 01:07:16.910 align:middle line:90%
the pause button.

01:07:16.910 --> 01:07:20.800 align:middle line:84%
Basically at MIT those are
usually enacted when safety

01:07:20.800 --> 01:07:21.780 align:middle line:90%
becomes an issue.

01:07:21.780 --> 01:07:25.170 align:middle line:90%
People are running too fast.

01:07:25.170 --> 01:07:27.650 align:middle line:84%
Somebody just fell down and you
want to be able to stop

01:07:27.650 --> 01:07:30.710 align:middle line:84%
everyone from running, and
so they shout halt.

01:07:30.710 --> 01:07:33.295 align:middle line:90%


01:07:33.295 --> 01:07:37.000 align:middle line:84%
Typically they're happening in
real time because it's really

01:07:37.000 --> 01:07:41.980 align:middle line:84%
hard to do turn based stuff that
doesn't quickly feel like

01:07:41.980 --> 01:07:42.390 align:middle line:90%
a board game.

01:07:42.390 --> 01:07:44.199 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I mean like, when you
say someone would want to

01:07:44.199 --> 01:07:46.260 align:middle line:84%
do a background check and you'd
say I don't know, come

01:07:46.260 --> 01:07:47.010 align:middle line:90%
back in two hours.

01:07:47.010 --> 01:07:49.080 align:middle line:84%
You'd literally wait
two hours?

01:07:49.080 --> 01:07:52.000 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: So, yeah,
often that really

01:07:52.000 --> 01:07:52.990 align:middle line:90%
means just two hours.

01:07:52.990 --> 01:07:55.530 align:middle line:84%
And that's an interesting thing
of that it's real time

01:07:55.530 --> 01:07:58.000 align:middle line:84%
but it's kind of a time delay
at the same time.

01:07:58.000 --> 01:08:03.060 align:middle line:84%
So as a game master you can
build in game mechanics that

01:08:03.060 --> 01:08:06.740 align:middle line:84%
take advantage of real time
delays to be able to give you

01:08:06.740 --> 01:08:09.920 align:middle line:84%
as game master some
time to breathe.

01:08:09.920 --> 01:08:11.900 align:middle line:84%
Typically they're happening
in real time.

01:08:11.900 --> 01:08:16.609 align:middle line:84%
I wrote a game where the clock
stopped somewhere between

01:08:16.609 --> 01:08:19.590 align:middle line:90%
midnight and 8:00 AM.

01:08:19.590 --> 01:08:22.965 align:middle line:84%
The game world literally froze,
well not literally

01:08:22.965 --> 01:08:24.215 align:middle line:90%
figuratively froze, as well.

01:08:24.215 --> 01:08:28.170 align:middle line:90%


01:08:28.170 --> 01:08:32.630 align:middle line:84%
And one minute after 8:00 the
clock would practically, in

01:08:32.630 --> 01:08:37.604 align:middle line:84%
game terms, have moved one
minute even though there were

01:08:37.604 --> 01:08:39.020 align:middle line:84%
eight intervening hours in
between so that people could

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:40.270 align:middle line:90%
go and take a break.

01:08:40.270 --> 01:08:42.990 align:middle line:90%


01:08:42.990 --> 01:08:45.220 align:middle line:84%
Which would be great
for an Army of

01:08:45.220 --> 01:08:47.460 align:middle line:90%
Darkness type of scenario.

01:08:47.460 --> 01:08:48.710 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:08:48.710 --> 01:08:50.439 align:middle line:90%


01:08:50.439 --> 01:08:52.770 align:middle line:84%
But those three films as opposed
to it all happened

01:08:52.770 --> 01:08:56.439 align:middle line:90%
within 48 hours.

01:08:56.439 --> 01:08:58.040 align:middle line:90%
So Andrew?

01:08:58.040 --> 01:08:59.200 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:08:59.200 --> 01:09:00.510 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I guess
two things, one

01:09:00.510 --> 01:09:02.120 align:middle line:90%
is in terms of wisdom.

01:09:02.120 --> 01:09:04.198 align:middle line:84%
Could you possibly have little
note cards that are flipped

01:09:04.198 --> 01:09:04.694 align:middle line:90%
over everywhere?

01:09:04.694 --> 01:09:06.678 align:middle line:84%
And then the wisdom person, the
person with high wisdom,

01:09:06.678 --> 01:09:09.700 align:middle line:84%
could read about information
in there?

01:09:09.700 --> 01:09:13.248 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You could certainly
have taken advantage of a

01:09:13.248 --> 01:09:14.020 align:middle line:84%
piece of paper taped on
the wall just put

01:09:14.020 --> 01:09:15.270 align:middle line:90%
in the back, right?

01:09:15.270 --> 01:09:16.800 align:middle line:90%


01:09:16.800 --> 01:09:18.936 align:middle line:84%
Some people can arguably be able
to read off the back of

01:09:18.936 --> 01:09:20.569 align:middle line:90%
the paper, some people aren't.

01:09:20.569 --> 01:09:23.960 align:middle line:84%
Which will be a little bit
easier than trying to keep it

01:09:23.960 --> 01:09:25.740 align:middle line:90%
all in your head at once.

01:09:25.740 --> 01:09:27.990 align:middle line:84%
You can also give very limited
wisdom, like the whole psychic

01:09:27.990 --> 01:09:30.050 align:middle line:90%
example, right?

01:09:30.050 --> 01:09:32.240 align:middle line:90%
I am sensitive in some way.

01:09:32.240 --> 01:09:35.850 align:middle line:84%
I can tell when certain
things are cursed.

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:39.920 align:middle line:84%
By giving them clues on printed
pieces of paper that

01:09:39.920 --> 01:09:42.569 align:middle line:84%
nobody else knows how
to interpret.

01:09:42.569 --> 01:09:47.249 align:middle line:84%
I see there is a number
associated with each one of

01:09:47.249 --> 01:09:47.410 align:middle line:90%
these rooms.

01:09:47.410 --> 01:09:49.580 align:middle line:84%
That number happens
to be even.

01:09:49.580 --> 01:09:53.029 align:middle line:84%
This is not a place that any
sane person would want to be.

01:09:53.029 --> 01:09:55.374 align:middle line:84%
And there are people who have
the wisdom to tell that and

01:09:55.374 --> 01:09:58.240 align:middle line:84%
there are some people
who don't.

01:09:58.240 --> 01:10:01.000 align:middle line:84%
That's another way to put those
environmental clues.

01:10:01.000 --> 01:10:06.010 align:middle line:90%


01:10:06.010 --> 01:10:12.510 align:middle line:84%
Particularly in MIT, hiding
clues in plain sight, and

01:10:12.510 --> 01:10:14.220 align:middle line:84%
every single way that
you can possibly do

01:10:14.220 --> 01:10:17.720 align:middle line:90%
that, is really exploited.

01:10:17.720 --> 01:10:20.080 align:middle line:90%
So the numbers for instance.

01:10:20.080 --> 01:10:24.130 align:middle line:84%
I've seen games where
everyone has five

01:10:24.130 --> 01:10:24.875 align:middle line:90%
digits on their badge.

01:10:24.875 --> 01:10:28.120 align:middle line:84%
And every single one of those
digits means something.

01:10:28.120 --> 01:10:33.805 align:middle line:84%
If you happen to have the
ability to interpret them, but

01:10:33.805 --> 01:10:36.910 align:middle line:84%
each one of the digits means
something different.

01:10:36.910 --> 01:10:40.736 align:middle line:90%
So that's an example.

01:10:40.736 --> 01:10:42.220 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: And just
really quickly.

01:10:42.220 --> 01:10:44.130 align:middle line:84%
In terms of space, how much
space do Assassin's Guild

01:10:44.130 --> 01:10:47.320 align:middle line:90%
games take?

01:10:47.320 --> 01:10:49.210 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: MIT Assassin's Guild
has the advantage of the

01:10:49.210 --> 01:10:50.210 align:middle line:90%
MIT campus.

01:10:50.210 --> 01:10:56.152 align:middle line:84%
So it can go anywhere from main
campus mounted by Mass

01:10:56.152 --> 01:11:02.600 align:middle line:84%
Ave. and Main Street,
to all over campus.

01:11:02.600 --> 01:11:04.740 align:middle line:90%
But players hate that.

01:11:04.740 --> 01:11:06.060 align:middle line:84%
Players hate walking
all over campus.

01:11:06.060 --> 01:11:07.680 align:middle line:90%
It kills their feet.

01:11:07.680 --> 01:11:12.770 align:middle line:84%
So often it just turns out to
be the few rooms that they

01:11:12.770 --> 01:11:15.820 align:middle line:84%
could get room reservations for,
the MIT campus activities

01:11:15.820 --> 01:11:18.770 align:middle line:84%
complex, and the sort of general
vicinity around there.

01:11:18.770 --> 01:11:21.520 align:middle line:84%
Outdoors is really not used
much, because MIT Assassin's

01:11:21.520 --> 01:11:24.270 align:middle line:84%
Guild loves shooting little
plastic things at each other.

01:11:24.270 --> 01:11:25.630 align:middle line:84%
And when the wind blows
that just sucks.

01:11:25.630 --> 01:11:28.908 align:middle line:90%


01:11:28.908 --> 01:11:29.884 align:middle line:90%
Jeremy?

01:11:29.884 --> 01:11:33.450 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do those eight
story [INAUDIBLE]

01:11:33.450 --> 01:11:37.135 align:middle line:84%
games that have gone way
horribly, fallen apart.

01:11:37.135 --> 01:11:39.576 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: Oh, absolutely.

01:11:39.576 --> 01:11:41.986 align:middle line:90%
How much time have we got?

01:11:41.986 --> 01:11:44.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Can you highlight
what are the mistakes that

01:11:44.560 --> 01:11:45.966 align:middle line:90%
have made--

01:11:45.966 --> 01:11:48.310 align:middle line:84%
everyone who's doing these,
especially on my team, they're

01:11:48.310 --> 01:11:48.880 align:middle line:90%
all smart people.

01:11:48.880 --> 01:11:50.980 align:middle line:84%
And they're written
by smart people.

01:11:50.980 --> 01:11:53.435 align:middle line:84%
So what hazard mistakes do smart
people still make that

01:11:53.435 --> 01:11:55.190 align:middle line:84%
results in these games
falling apart?

01:11:55.190 --> 01:11:57.350 align:middle line:90%
Or bad things happen?

01:11:57.350 --> 01:11:59.020 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Well, the most common
thing that happens is

01:11:59.020 --> 01:12:03.760 align:middle line:84%
not even an issue of how smart
the game writers were but how

01:12:03.760 --> 01:12:05.033 align:middle line:84%
much experience they've
had in seeing

01:12:05.033 --> 01:12:08.345 align:middle line:90%
things go horribly wrong.

01:12:08.345 --> 01:12:09.570 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Right, which
is why I'm asking.

01:12:09.570 --> 01:12:12.180 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Just like this class
has given you experience

01:12:12.180 --> 01:12:15.420 align:middle line:84%
making games, you've seen how
some projects can go wrong.

01:12:15.420 --> 01:12:20.030 align:middle line:84%
A lot of it is just system
dynamics, right?

01:12:20.030 --> 01:12:21.490 align:middle line:84%
I put together a
bunch of rules.

01:12:21.490 --> 01:12:24.540 align:middle line:84%
I didn't expect those rules
to interact in that way.

01:12:24.540 --> 01:12:28.090 align:middle line:84%
But because a live-action game
is difficult to run multiple

01:12:28.090 --> 01:12:31.460 align:middle line:84%
times, the first
time you see--

01:12:31.460 --> 01:12:33.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: How do you play
a live-action game

01:12:33.020 --> 01:12:35.040 align:middle line:90%
that lasts to a month?

01:12:35.040 --> 01:12:36.090 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: That actually came
up in the forums I believe.

01:12:36.090 --> 01:12:38.950 align:middle line:84%
Somebody was asking
about this.

01:12:38.950 --> 01:12:41.650 align:middle line:90%
You can at best to a test--

01:12:41.650 --> 01:12:42.870 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Give them mechanics?

01:12:42.870 --> 01:12:44.280 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: --You can play the
sort of game mechanics.

01:12:44.280 --> 01:12:46.860 align:middle line:84%
Write down the stripped down
version which takes out all of

01:12:46.860 --> 01:12:50.030 align:middle line:84%
the plot motivations and
just has the combat.

01:12:50.030 --> 01:12:51.150 align:middle line:90%
This is how combat plays.

01:12:51.150 --> 01:12:53.160 align:middle line:84%
I did that for a Matrix
game once.

01:12:53.160 --> 01:12:55.280 align:middle line:84%
And it's like, I'm writing
a game of a Matrix.

01:12:55.280 --> 01:12:58.860 align:middle line:84%
Combat is really freaking
important.

01:12:58.860 --> 01:13:02.960 align:middle line:84%
So I just made an entire game
where all it was, you want to

01:13:02.960 --> 01:13:05.030 align:middle line:84%
go in and get this thing
and get out.

01:13:05.030 --> 01:13:06.110 align:middle line:90%
That was the whole rule.

01:13:06.110 --> 01:13:08.880 align:middle line:84%
And here's your three pages
of contact notes.

01:13:08.880 --> 01:13:10.170 align:middle line:90%
And people were like holy shit,

01:13:10.170 --> 01:13:11.420 align:middle line:90%
this is a lot to remember.

01:13:11.420 --> 01:13:13.770 align:middle line:90%


01:13:13.770 --> 01:13:16.950 align:middle line:84%
And that showed me the
problems that I had.

01:13:16.950 --> 01:13:20.420 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, it wasn't so
catastrophic that I would use

01:13:20.420 --> 01:13:22.284 align:middle line:84%
it as a catastrophic
game example.

01:13:22.284 --> 01:13:24.100 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Tell us about
a catastrophic.

01:13:24.100 --> 01:13:27.250 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Catastrophic
game examples.

01:13:27.250 --> 01:13:27.610 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:13:27.610 --> 01:13:29.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Tell us someone
tripped and hurt themselves.

01:13:29.090 --> 01:13:29.890 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:13:29.890 --> 01:13:35.117 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: No, that's not what
you're looking for I

01:13:35.117 --> 01:13:35.710 align:middle line:90%
understand.

01:13:35.710 --> 01:13:43.580 align:middle line:84%
It was a game where everyone
was playing undead.

01:13:43.580 --> 01:13:44.950 align:middle line:90%
I think they were vampires.

01:13:44.950 --> 01:13:49.810 align:middle line:84%
So they could get knocked down
if they got shot, and they

01:13:49.810 --> 01:13:51.420 align:middle line:84%
would get up again
in five minutes

01:13:51.420 --> 01:13:52.260 align:middle line:90%
because they were immortal.

01:13:52.260 --> 01:13:54.247 align:middle line:84%
Unless they got staked
or something they

01:13:54.247 --> 01:13:55.980 align:middle line:90%
would get up again.

01:13:55.980 --> 01:13:58.530 align:middle line:84%
Unfortunately, there were a lot
of vampires in the game.

01:13:58.530 --> 01:14:01.790 align:middle line:84%
Often many of them were
in different factions.

01:14:01.790 --> 01:14:04.940 align:middle line:84%
So they were going after
each other with guns.

01:14:04.940 --> 01:14:08.350 align:middle line:84%
So when two people who were
obviously against each other

01:14:08.350 --> 01:14:11.830 align:middle line:84%
shot each other, fell over,
they would get up in five

01:14:11.830 --> 01:14:13.456 align:middle line:84%
minutes and shoot each
other again.

01:14:13.456 --> 01:14:16.860 align:middle line:90%


01:14:16.860 --> 01:14:23.035 align:middle line:84%
Repeatedly begging for someone
to show up and do something to

01:14:23.035 --> 01:14:25.060 align:middle line:90%
break this deadlock.

01:14:25.060 --> 01:14:26.910 align:middle line:84%
And that's one of those things
where it's like game

01:14:26.910 --> 01:14:28.553 align:middle line:84%
mechanically you know
what's supposed to

01:14:28.553 --> 01:14:29.910 align:middle line:90%
happen, and it sucks.

01:14:29.910 --> 01:14:34.180 align:middle line:90%


01:14:34.180 --> 01:14:35.140 align:middle line:90%
So that's one example.

01:14:35.140 --> 01:14:36.560 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do we have to
wait five minutes?

01:14:36.560 --> 01:14:39.960 align:middle line:84%
Can we just continue to
shoot each other?

01:14:39.960 --> 01:14:42.944 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: But the issue is,
again, these games are

01:14:42.944 --> 01:14:43.590 align:middle line:90%
happening in real time.

01:14:43.590 --> 01:14:46.635 align:middle line:84%
So the five minute rule is there
possibly for a reason.

01:14:46.635 --> 01:14:49.320 align:middle line:90%


01:14:49.320 --> 01:14:52.320 align:middle line:84%
While you are down for five
minutes those five minutes

01:14:52.320 --> 01:14:54.870 align:middle line:84%
become very valuable for someone
who wants you down for

01:14:54.870 --> 01:14:56.120 align:middle line:90%
those five minutes.

01:14:56.120 --> 01:14:57.940 align:middle line:90%


01:14:57.940 --> 01:14:58.300 align:middle line:90%
You can kill me.

01:14:58.300 --> 01:14:59.240 align:middle line:90%
I can kill you.

01:14:59.240 --> 01:15:01.250 align:middle line:84%
But both of us can knock each
other down for five minutes.

01:15:01.250 --> 01:15:02.920 align:middle line:84%
And that's a great
delaying tactic.

01:15:02.920 --> 01:15:05.640 align:middle line:84%
And I can send one of my friends
and do something that

01:15:05.640 --> 01:15:08.640 align:middle line:84%
I don't want you interfering
with.

01:15:08.640 --> 01:15:10.120 align:middle line:84%
And if that's the case
then that's great.

01:15:10.120 --> 01:15:12.220 align:middle line:84%
But often it was just, oh
crap there's no way

01:15:12.220 --> 01:15:13.572 align:middle line:90%
to break the deadlock.

01:15:13.572 --> 01:15:15.620 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: You'd figure the
limiting factor would be

01:15:15.620 --> 01:15:17.280 align:middle line:90%
ammunition though.

01:15:17.280 --> 01:15:19.070 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I think that game
actually let you pick up your

01:15:19.070 --> 01:15:20.320 align:middle line:90%
ammunition all you want.

01:15:20.320 --> 01:15:23.260 align:middle line:90%


01:15:23.260 --> 01:15:25.960 align:middle line:84%
Because it can be a really
annoying limitation to run out

01:15:25.960 --> 01:15:26.990 align:middle line:90%
of ammunition.

01:15:26.990 --> 01:15:29.315 align:middle line:84%
Especially if you are playing
with the little rubber dart

01:15:29.315 --> 01:15:31.970 align:middle line:84%
guns because carrying
a whole pile of

01:15:31.970 --> 01:15:32.550 align:middle line:90%
them is really annoying.

01:15:32.550 --> 01:15:37.090 align:middle line:84%
So if the rule is, I get to pick
them up, then you have

01:15:37.090 --> 01:15:39.176 align:middle line:84%
the allusion of having a lot of
ammunition without having

01:15:39.176 --> 01:15:43.160 align:middle line:90%
to carry a lot of ammunition.

01:15:43.160 --> 01:15:45.290 align:middle line:84%
So that's one example of
a catastrophic failure.

01:15:45.290 --> 01:15:48.720 align:middle line:84%
There's a really funny quote
regarding brainwashing

01:15:48.720 --> 01:15:55.160 align:middle line:84%
actually, which is
where were you?

01:15:55.160 --> 01:15:56.830 align:middle line:90%
I was not being brainwashed.

01:15:56.830 --> 01:15:58.440 align:middle line:90%
Who were you with?

01:15:58.440 --> 01:16:01.625 align:middle line:84%
Not with the people who
were brainwashing me.

01:16:01.625 --> 01:16:04.200 align:middle line:84%
Back to the problem with
the persuasion, right?

01:16:04.200 --> 01:16:07.900 align:middle line:84%
It's like, I want to tell you
something because my goals

01:16:07.900 --> 01:16:10.950 align:middle line:84%
haven't changed far enough
to want to align

01:16:10.950 --> 01:16:12.240 align:middle line:90%
me to my new goals.

01:16:12.240 --> 01:16:14.190 align:middle line:84%
So I'm going to do everything
that I can without

01:16:14.190 --> 01:16:15.160 align:middle line:90%
breaking the rules.

01:16:15.160 --> 01:16:17.590 align:middle line:84%
I'm not allowed to tell you that
I was being brainwashed.

01:16:17.590 --> 01:16:19.236 align:middle line:84%
So I can say that I wasn't
being brainwashed.

01:16:19.236 --> 01:16:23.470 align:middle line:90%


01:16:23.470 --> 01:16:24.720 align:middle line:90%
Other catastrophic?

01:16:24.720 --> 01:16:26.800 align:middle line:90%


01:16:26.800 --> 01:16:29.132 align:middle line:84%
How long do you think the thing
will take probably?

01:16:29.132 --> 01:16:30.545 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: I don't know.

01:16:30.545 --> 01:16:33.371 align:middle line:84%
Probably about as long
as a game of Mafia.

01:16:33.371 --> 01:16:35.740 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: OK, so like
half an hour?

01:16:35.740 --> 01:16:37.010 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Depends
on the people.

01:16:37.010 --> 01:16:41.040 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you put a time
limit on discussions then,

01:16:41.040 --> 01:16:42.960 align:middle line:84%
because I've played in the past
where it's lasted hours.

01:16:42.960 --> 01:16:45.530 align:middle line:84%
Because there's no cap and
people just went into these

01:16:45.530 --> 01:16:48.160 align:middle line:84%
long discussions in
between rounds.

01:16:48.160 --> 01:16:50.730 align:middle line:84%
So if you put a cap like OK,
it's going to be this long a

01:16:50.730 --> 01:16:52.270 align:middle line:84%
discussion and then we're going
to kill someone off.

01:16:52.270 --> 01:16:55.250 align:middle line:90%


01:16:55.250 --> 01:16:57.540 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Let's see, I
had wanted to read.

01:16:57.540 --> 01:16:58.830 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Played online Mafia?

01:16:58.830 --> 01:17:01.535 align:middle line:84%
It's a totally different
experience.

01:17:01.535 --> 01:17:02.990 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: Chat involved
or something?

01:17:02.990 --> 01:17:04.240 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Forums usually.

01:17:04.240 --> 01:17:09.300 align:middle line:90%


01:17:09.300 --> 01:17:11.250 align:middle line:84%
So basically, when you can look
at the history of what

01:17:11.250 --> 01:17:12.975 align:middle line:84%
people have said and who they've
accused and what order

01:17:12.975 --> 01:17:15.350 align:middle line:84%
they did it in, it completely
changes how

01:17:15.350 --> 01:17:17.730 align:middle line:90%
Mafia turns out, right?

01:17:17.730 --> 01:17:20.362 align:middle line:84%
Because all of a sudden, people
start going, back in

01:17:20.362 --> 01:17:23.890 align:middle line:84%
the history and compiling
statistics [INAUDIBLE].

01:17:23.890 --> 01:17:26.445 align:middle line:84%
And who said what and then who
must be on their side if

01:17:26.445 --> 01:17:28.030 align:middle line:90%
that's the case.

01:17:28.030 --> 01:17:30.755 align:middle line:84%
But there's this kind of meta
knowledge that you can use

01:17:30.755 --> 01:17:37.030 align:middle line:84%
with this if you are Mafia
because you can tell

01:17:37.030 --> 01:17:38.280 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:17:38.280 --> 01:17:41.085 align:middle line:90%


01:17:41.085 --> 01:17:43.770 align:middle line:84%
We were able to communicate
at the Mafia privately.

01:17:43.770 --> 01:17:46.910 align:middle line:90%


01:17:46.910 --> 01:17:49.267 align:middle line:84%
That meant we could for instance
do things like, make

01:17:49.267 --> 01:17:52.440 align:middle line:84%
sure we were never the first
people to accuse anyone.

01:17:52.440 --> 01:17:54.490 align:middle line:84%
So that's a Mafia truth,
never do that.

01:17:54.490 --> 01:17:57.415 align:middle line:84%
Because it immediately makes you
look suspicious later on

01:17:57.415 --> 01:18:01.320 align:middle line:84%
if somebody turns out
to be the Mafia.

01:18:01.320 --> 01:18:04.280 align:middle line:84%
There's no way that you can be
the first person who accuses.

01:18:04.280 --> 01:18:07.070 align:middle line:84%
But bandwagoning somebody who's
not in the Mafia is

01:18:07.070 --> 01:18:08.940 align:middle line:90%
always great.

01:18:08.940 --> 01:18:13.040 align:middle line:84%
So you can coordinate to do the
sort of thing to try and

01:18:13.040 --> 01:18:15.090 align:middle line:84%
defeat the statistical
analysis that

01:18:15.090 --> 01:18:16.740 align:middle line:90%
somebody else is doing.

01:18:16.740 --> 01:18:17.530 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: You've
got this whole

01:18:17.530 --> 01:18:18.970 align:middle line:90%
shadow governance theory.

01:18:18.970 --> 01:18:23.670 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: That's a great
[INAUDIBLE] tactic to try and

01:18:23.670 --> 01:18:26.575 align:middle line:84%
get you away from my team
is to actually accuse

01:18:26.575 --> 01:18:28.020 align:middle line:90%
someone on your team.

01:18:28.020 --> 01:18:33.180 align:middle line:84%
And hope that they don't
actually vote them off.

01:18:33.180 --> 01:18:36.825 align:middle line:84%
I've definitely been in a game
where we purposely killed off

01:18:36.825 --> 01:18:39.180 align:middle line:84%
one of the people on our
team just so that

01:18:39.180 --> 01:18:42.580 align:middle line:90%
we wouldn't be accused.

01:18:42.580 --> 01:18:46.860 align:middle line:84%
Oh, he cannot be on Mafia
because he killed off a Mafia.

01:18:46.860 --> 01:18:50.620 align:middle line:84%
OK, and then we end up winning
because no one

01:18:50.620 --> 01:18:52.190 align:middle line:90%
figured that out.

01:18:52.190 --> 01:18:55.080 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I mean, that's one
of the best things about all

01:18:55.080 --> 01:18:58.480 align:middle line:84%
kinds of live-action games is
that it almost encourages

01:18:58.480 --> 01:19:00.980 align:middle line:84%
people to look for
these slightly

01:19:00.980 --> 01:19:02.594 align:middle line:90%
out of the box illusions.

01:19:02.594 --> 01:19:05.029 align:middle line:84%
These sort of emergent I'm not
really going to expect that

01:19:05.029 --> 01:19:06.330 align:middle line:84%
but it still fits within
the rules.

01:19:06.330 --> 01:19:08.420 align:middle line:84%
Because we are dealing with real
people for the most part

01:19:08.420 --> 01:19:11.098 align:middle line:84%
and you're not limited to the
cards you're allowed to play

01:19:11.098 --> 01:19:13.014 align:middle line:84%
or the turns you're
allowed to take.

01:19:13.014 --> 01:19:14.264 align:middle line:90%
So taking advantage of that.

01:19:14.264 --> 01:19:17.330 align:middle line:90%


01:19:17.330 --> 01:19:21.520 align:middle line:84%
I have one more disaster
story.

01:19:21.520 --> 01:19:24.520 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: I want to hear it.

01:19:24.520 --> 01:19:26.150 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: I'll limit
it to just one for

01:19:26.150 --> 01:19:27.400 align:middle line:90%
the people who don't.

01:19:27.400 --> 01:19:29.430 align:middle line:90%


01:19:29.430 --> 01:19:31.360 align:middle line:84%
This was a game where
there was a

01:19:31.360 --> 01:19:33.940 align:middle line:90%
virus that was spreading.

01:19:33.940 --> 01:19:36.940 align:middle line:84%
People who are in Assassin's
Guild might have

01:19:36.940 --> 01:19:38.460 align:middle line:90%
already heard this one.

01:19:38.460 --> 01:19:43.140 align:middle line:84%
The whole basic idea is that
there was a explosive

01:19:43.140 --> 01:19:44.850 align:middle line:90%
nano-virus.

01:19:44.850 --> 01:19:48.515 align:middle line:84%
People who got infected after a
certain amount of time would

01:19:48.515 --> 01:19:52.120 align:middle line:84%
explode and anybody in
the room with them

01:19:52.120 --> 01:19:54.880 align:middle line:90%
would now be infected.

01:19:54.880 --> 01:19:57.354 align:middle line:90%
Of course this was a game--

01:19:57.354 --> 01:19:59.540 align:middle line:84%
a game set in science fiction
kind of thing--

01:19:59.540 --> 01:20:02.430 align:middle line:84%
and we've got doctors
who are capable.

01:20:02.430 --> 01:20:06.830 align:middle line:84%
Who have mechanics to be
able to research cures.

01:20:06.830 --> 01:20:08.570 align:middle line:84%
However when the doctor happened
to be one of the

01:20:08.570 --> 01:20:14.760 align:middle line:84%
first people to die, what
rapidly happens is that what

01:20:14.760 --> 01:20:18.545 align:middle line:84%
should have been a one night
game turned out to be a one

01:20:18.545 --> 01:20:19.795 align:middle line:84%
hour game and everybody
exploded.

01:20:19.795 --> 01:20:27.690 align:middle line:90%


01:20:27.690 --> 01:20:29.800 align:middle line:84%
I guess that might come
down to feasibility.

01:20:29.800 --> 01:20:33.530 align:middle line:84%
But you can think about it as
you need a certain amount of

01:20:33.530 --> 01:20:36.800 align:middle line:84%
redundancy when you're designing
any game, but

01:20:36.800 --> 01:20:38.400 align:middle line:84%
particularly live-actions
games.

01:20:38.400 --> 01:20:41.050 align:middle line:90%
Because shit happens.

01:20:41.050 --> 01:20:43.920 align:middle line:84%
And you've got to expect
that to happen.

01:20:43.920 --> 01:20:46.100 align:middle line:84%
There's always this fear about,
oh I put in too many

01:20:46.100 --> 01:20:47.900 align:middle line:84%
resources into this game and the
players are going to find

01:20:47.900 --> 01:20:48.950 align:middle line:90%
it too easy.

01:20:48.950 --> 01:20:52.000 align:middle line:84%
And more often than not, yeah,
there's some players that that

01:20:52.000 --> 01:20:53.830 align:middle line:84%
are going to say we breezed
through that.

01:20:53.830 --> 01:20:57.125 align:middle line:84%
But, rarely do people get
terribly upset in a

01:20:57.125 --> 01:20:59.900 align:middle line:84%
live-action game when something
is really too easy.

01:20:59.900 --> 01:21:03.798 align:middle line:84%
They get really upset when it
comes to the end and it seems

01:21:03.798 --> 01:21:05.470 align:middle line:90%
to be impossible.

01:21:05.470 --> 01:21:11.410 align:middle line:84%
Oh, so the GMs only made it
possible for one person to fix

01:21:11.410 --> 01:21:12.070 align:middle line:90%
this problem?

01:21:12.070 --> 01:21:15.330 align:middle line:84%
And they didn't think about that
person just dying in the

01:21:15.330 --> 01:21:17.440 align:middle line:90%
beginning of the game?

01:21:17.440 --> 01:21:20.730 align:middle line:90%
So that was another example.

01:21:20.730 --> 01:21:22.240 align:middle line:90%
That [INAUDIBLE], I'm sorry.

01:21:22.240 --> 01:21:25.476 align:middle line:84%
I feel really bad about bringing
up examples from my

01:21:25.476 --> 01:21:29.016 align:middle line:84%
games, but I don't know which
ones are the ones that I

01:21:29.016 --> 01:21:29.650 align:middle line:90%
genuinely--

01:21:29.650 --> 01:21:32.530 align:middle line:90%
Oh, I do have one more.

01:21:32.530 --> 01:21:34.380 align:middle line:84%
So, this was after The
Sims came out.

01:21:34.380 --> 01:21:38.960 align:middle line:84%
And people should really
be worried about that.

01:21:38.960 --> 01:21:40.860 align:middle line:84%
I thought having a bladder stat
would be a really good

01:21:40.860 --> 01:21:42.110 align:middle line:90%
idea for a game.

01:21:42.110 --> 01:21:45.520 align:middle line:90%


01:21:45.520 --> 01:21:47.033 align:middle line:84%
This was a game that
was set in a train

01:21:47.033 --> 01:21:47.490 align:middle line:90%
that was steam pumped.

01:21:47.490 --> 01:21:49.120 align:middle line:90%
So tea was the game.

01:21:49.120 --> 01:21:50.990 align:middle line:84%
Tea was in fact the
combat drug.

01:21:50.990 --> 01:21:54.520 align:middle line:84%
If you drank tea, your combat
status would increase but so

01:21:54.520 --> 01:21:56.770 align:middle line:90%
would your bladder stat.

01:21:56.770 --> 01:21:59.670 align:middle line:84%
Once your bladder stat was
passed a certain point all

01:21:59.670 --> 01:22:02.730 align:middle line:84%
your combat stats went down and
you really had to go to

01:22:02.730 --> 01:22:04.470 align:middle line:90%
the restroom.

01:22:04.470 --> 01:22:08.200 align:middle line:84%
Which was really interesting,
most people just

01:22:08.200 --> 01:22:10.970 align:middle line:90%
reviled that game.

01:22:10.970 --> 01:22:14.800 align:middle line:84%
Because there was one more
number they had to hit that

01:22:14.800 --> 01:22:19.490 align:middle line:84%
was stupid annoying and really
didn't do terribly much for

01:22:19.490 --> 01:22:21.120 align:middle line:90%
the immersion of the game.

01:22:21.120 --> 01:22:24.100 align:middle line:84%
I was thinking, it's a game
about travelling on the train.

01:22:24.100 --> 01:22:25.360 align:middle line:84%
So you use the restroom
all the time.

01:22:25.360 --> 01:22:26.905 align:middle line:84%
That's the only thing I do
when I'm travelling.

01:22:26.905 --> 01:22:29.490 align:middle line:90%
I use the restroom.

01:22:29.490 --> 01:22:30.740 align:middle line:90%
[INAUDIBLE].

01:22:30.740 --> 01:22:33.870 align:middle line:90%


01:22:33.870 --> 01:22:35.600 align:middle line:84%
But that led to some interesting
emergent

01:22:35.600 --> 01:22:36.740 align:middle line:90%
situations.

01:22:36.740 --> 01:22:39.380 align:middle line:84%
Because when this train gets
attacked, and everybody needs

01:22:39.380 --> 01:22:42.350 align:middle line:84%
to find a safe place to hide,
what they all do is they head

01:22:42.350 --> 01:22:46.690 align:middle line:84%
into the washroom, barricade
it, and drink tea until all

01:22:46.690 --> 01:22:50.595 align:middle line:84%
their combat stats go out the
roof and then win the fight.

01:22:50.595 --> 01:22:53.020 align:middle line:84%
Because they were right there
in the restroom they could

01:22:53.020 --> 01:22:54.960 align:middle line:90%
afford to do that.

01:22:54.960 --> 01:22:56.210 align:middle line:90%
It was hilarious.

01:22:56.210 --> 01:22:59.325 align:middle line:90%


01:22:59.325 --> 01:23:03.750 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Some of these people
come up with exploits.

01:23:03.750 --> 01:23:05.270 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: They may hate the
rules, but once they

01:23:05.270 --> 01:23:07.080 align:middle line:84%
understand how the systems work
they're going to come up

01:23:07.080 --> 01:23:09.270 align:middle line:84%
with ways to use it to
their advantage.

01:23:09.270 --> 01:23:12.622 align:middle line:84%
And that can be some of the
most enjoyable moments.

01:23:12.622 --> 01:23:15.274 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I saw a squirrel
this past weekend, and he

01:23:15.274 --> 01:23:20.942 align:middle line:84%
figured out if he stood under a
bird feeding thing up in the

01:23:20.942 --> 01:23:24.562 align:middle line:84%
tree it could just get free food
because when the birds

01:23:24.562 --> 01:23:26.724 align:middle line:84%
ate it once every two to three
minutes they would drop a

01:23:26.724 --> 01:23:27.556 align:middle line:90%
grain inevitably.

01:23:27.556 --> 01:23:32.047 align:middle line:84%
And there was this really fat
squirrel just sitting there.

01:23:32.047 --> 01:23:36.890 align:middle line:84%
He spent the entire day sitting
right [INAUDIBLE].

01:23:36.890 --> 01:23:38.852 align:middle line:84%
So it's like it's so natural
to try and find exploits of

01:23:38.852 --> 01:23:40.820 align:middle line:84%
everything and work it
to your advantage.

01:23:40.820 --> 01:23:41.920 align:middle line:90%
PROFESSOR: There is a moral--

01:23:41.920 --> 01:23:43.113 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: It doesn't work
for [INAUDIBLE] but

01:23:43.113 --> 01:23:44.310 align:middle line:90%
you still do it anyway.

01:23:44.310 --> 01:23:46.140 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: There is
a moral to that.

01:23:46.140 --> 01:23:49.620 align:middle line:84%
Is that the live-action games,
especially if you're playing

01:23:49.620 --> 01:23:53.090 align:middle line:84%
in the live-action game
community, there is whatever

01:23:53.090 --> 01:23:56.776 align:middle line:84%
you write down as a rule we put
it up on the wall and they

01:23:56.776 --> 01:23:58.270 align:middle line:90%
put it on a piece of paper.

01:23:58.270 --> 01:23:59.880 align:middle line:84%
Someone's going to
try to do it.

01:23:59.880 --> 01:24:04.904 align:middle line:84%
And you have to think of, I'm
going to put in a rule that's

01:24:04.904 --> 01:24:08.324 align:middle line:84%
so ridiculous that people are
not going to want to try and

01:24:08.324 --> 01:24:10.980 align:middle line:84%
do it because it's going to
hurt them or something.

01:24:10.980 --> 01:24:14.098 align:middle line:84%
Like jump on this spot
100 times and you

01:24:14.098 --> 01:24:14.930 align:middle line:90%
will unlock this door.

01:24:14.930 --> 01:24:18.220 align:middle line:84%
Or you could find someone with
lock picking, and you'll just

01:24:18.220 --> 01:24:19.196 align:middle line:84%
assume that person's
going to find

01:24:19.196 --> 01:24:20.490 align:middle line:90%
someone with lock picking.

01:24:20.490 --> 01:24:22.980 align:middle line:84%
No, someone's going to jump on
the spot 100 times and they're

01:24:22.980 --> 01:24:25.160 align:middle line:84%
going to hate you for
it because you

01:24:25.160 --> 01:24:26.300 align:middle line:90%
put the rule in there.

01:24:26.300 --> 01:24:32.530 align:middle line:84%
So try not to hurt your players,
would be the moral,

01:24:32.530 --> 01:24:34.115 align:middle line:84%
because they will do what
you tell them to do.

01:24:34.115 --> 01:24:37.680 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Do you guys sign
disclosure forms?

01:24:37.680 --> 01:24:39.116 align:middle line:84%
PROFESSOR: All right, that's
a [INAUDIBLE].

01:24:39.116 --> 01:24:42.246 align:middle line:90%


01:24:42.246 --> 01:24:44.947 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: Before we go,
I want to just clarify this

01:24:44.947 --> 01:24:46.420 align:middle line:84%
version of the investigation
mechanic.

01:24:46.420 --> 01:24:52.390 align:middle line:84%
So, the group of scientists
gets to test two people.

01:24:52.390 --> 01:24:56.430 align:middle line:84%
If you catch the thing then
they're dead and out.

01:24:56.430 --> 01:24:58.600 align:middle line:84%
If you catch them on the second
time-- this is what

01:24:58.600 --> 01:25:00.690 align:middle line:84%
I've read-- if you catch them
on second guess you get

01:25:00.690 --> 01:25:03.030 align:middle line:90%
another guess.

01:25:03.030 --> 01:25:04.764 align:middle line:84%
I'm not sure if that
means you can have

01:25:04.764 --> 01:25:06.210 align:middle line:90%
infinite guesses or not.

01:25:06.210 --> 01:25:07.180 align:middle line:84%
So that's something to
play around with.

01:25:07.180 --> 01:25:09.180 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: If you test the wrong
person who's not the

01:25:09.180 --> 01:25:09.780 align:middle line:90%
thing they don't die?

01:25:09.780 --> 01:25:12.660 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: No.

01:25:12.660 --> 01:25:14.122 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So I test someone the
first time, they're the

01:25:14.122 --> 01:25:15.430 align:middle line:90%
thing, then they're out.

01:25:15.430 --> 01:25:17.384 align:middle line:84%
But I test someone the second
time, regardless of whether or

01:25:17.384 --> 01:25:19.170 align:middle line:84%
not I tested a thing
the first time.

01:25:19.170 --> 01:25:19.990 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: Yes.

01:25:19.990 --> 01:25:22.370 align:middle line:84%
So you always get at
least two guesses.

01:25:22.370 --> 01:25:23.420 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: But if you test
someone on the second

01:25:23.420 --> 01:25:25.206 align:middle line:90%
one they don't die?

01:25:25.206 --> 01:25:26.870 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: If they're
the thing they're dead.

01:25:26.870 --> 01:25:27.830 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: The thing dies.

01:25:27.830 --> 01:25:29.680 align:middle line:84%
If you test someone on the first
time and they're the

01:25:29.680 --> 01:25:31.210 align:middle line:84%
thing you don't get
an extra guess?

01:25:31.210 --> 01:25:32.025 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Well, not
necessarily.

01:25:32.025 --> 01:25:33.320 align:middle line:90%
GUEST SPEAKER: You get two.

01:25:33.320 --> 01:25:35.420 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: You always get two.

01:25:35.420 --> 01:25:37.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: So if you guess
any time [INAUDIBLE]

01:25:37.720 --> 01:25:40.770 align:middle line:90%


01:25:40.770 --> 01:25:42.425 align:middle line:84%
but if you guess a thing
and then not a

01:25:42.425 --> 01:25:43.560 align:middle line:90%
thing you get a player.

01:25:43.560 --> 01:25:44.090 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think you
can catch the

01:25:44.090 --> 01:25:45.366 align:middle line:90%
thing really quickly.

01:25:45.366 --> 01:25:47.245 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: I think you can
catch the thing without

01:25:47.245 --> 01:25:50.575 align:middle line:84%
actually doing anything sort of
like making a system where

01:25:50.575 --> 01:25:52.350 align:middle line:84%
you automatically
catch the thing.

01:25:52.350 --> 01:25:56.200 align:middle line:84%
You're getting two guesses so
in the ratio of randomly

01:25:56.200 --> 01:25:56.960 align:middle line:90%
guessing you'll--

01:25:56.960 --> 01:25:59.490 align:middle line:84%
GUEST SPEAKER: And you can
certainly change it right?

01:25:59.490 --> 01:26:00.750 align:middle line:90%
This is just what I've found.

01:26:00.750 --> 01:26:02.710 align:middle line:84%
And it seemed like an
interesting thing, the whole

01:26:02.710 --> 01:26:04.160 align:middle line:90%
point of the part was.

01:26:04.160 --> 01:26:05.660 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Which thing
do you start with?

01:26:05.660 --> 01:26:07.020 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: No, you're
not [INAUDIBLE].

01:26:07.020 --> 01:26:10.060 align:middle line:84%
Because as I recall you would
infect someone first.

01:26:10.060 --> 01:26:11.984 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: Because you're going
to have two unprescribed.

01:26:11.984 --> 01:26:14.940 align:middle line:90%


01:26:14.940 --> 01:26:18.720 align:middle line:84%
AUDIENCE: This seems really
like biased towards

01:26:18.720 --> 01:26:20.330 align:middle line:90%
non-infected.

01:26:20.330 --> 01:26:21.580 align:middle line:90%
AUDIENCE: Well we'll find out.

01:26:21.580 --> 01:26:22.340 align:middle line:90%